/* AUTO-GENERATED by tools/parse-paths-md.js — do not edit by hand. */
const PATHS_STORY = {"mainline":{"id":"mainline","title":"The Paths","cast":["Cassia","Clare","Cohort","Crew Member","Dean","Ethan","Faye","Hosea","Jael","Jenny","Kye","Marcus","Margolis","Mason","Mira","Pearl","Rhys","Rowan","Rowing Boy","Sealyra","Shiro","Thea","Theo","Veda","Vivienne","Woman","Wren"],"chapters":[{"n":1,"title":"The Glass Elevator","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The service elevator at Meridian Capital is mirrored on three of its four faces, a configuration that obliges sixteen commuters, compressed shoulder to shoulder at seven forty-two each morning, to confront their own reflected proximity while sustaining the collective fiction that no one is breathing onto anyone else’s neck. Wedged behind your left shoulder, the facilities attendant delivers his customary unsolicited commentary on the building’s infrastructure."},{"k":"s","who":"Marcus","t":"“The glass elevator is sanitised twice a day. A German microfibre cloth, forty yuan a sheet, deployed once and discarded. The annual expenditure on elevator cloth exceeds my salary. I have resolved not to take it personally.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Theo","t":"He hoists his bag above his head. “And how is that resolution holding up.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Marcus","t":"“It is a daily discipline. Certain mornings I attain serenity. This is not one of them.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The doors disgorge their cargo onto the sixth floor, a corridor engineered to communicate affluence without stooping to announce it: carpet deep enough to absorb the report of a woman’s heels, illumination calibrated to the warm register that persuades consequential people they are among their own."},{"k":"n","t":"At the rearmost row, a forwarded message occupies the head of your inbox. SCHOLARS PROGRAM: INTERNAL NOMINATIONS OPEN. A fully funded further-education scheme routed through the adult-admissions track at Concord University, contingent upon the endorsement of a director or above."},{"k":"n","t":"You read the line once. Your posture recalibrates by a measurable degree."},{"k":"s","who":"Ethan","t":"He does not lift his eyes from the apparatus through which he is decanting his coffee. “You have seen the Scholars email. Your spine reorganised itself the instant the page resolved. Do not insult me by attributing it to the vendor invoices.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I am reading it a second time. That is still permitted in this building, I assume.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Ethan","t":"“Apply or refrain. But spare us both the performance of idle curiosity about a thing you plainly want enough to forfeit sleep over.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I require a director’s signature. I have occupied this floor for three weeks. I possess no director who would append their name to a lunch order on my behalf, let alone a nomination form.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Ethan","t":"The coffee descends to the desk with ceremony. “Correct. You do not. Which constitutes the solitary defect in your plan, and, not coincidentally, the only feature of it I consider worth observing.”"},{"k":"n","t":"A disturbance propagates through the atrium below before any single individual has consciously elected to look up. The glass elevator is descending."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"A boy of perhaps eighteen, tall in the manner of adolescents whose stature has outrun the skeleton tasked with supporting it. The charcoal suit fits the way water conforms to the vessel that contains it. His hair is silver beneath the halogen wash. His thumb traverses the surface of his phone in slow deliberate increments, and across the entire descent his gaze does not once ascend to acknowledge the building assembled to watch him."},{"k":"s","who":"Woman","t":"On the third-floor walkway, in the volume people mistake for confidential. “Every morning I watch that box descend and conclude, with renewed conviction, that he is not a verifiable human being.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The boy crosses the marble below without conceding it a glance, turns toward the investment director’s suite, and is gone."},{"k":"n","t":"You go to the printer. A woman in a grey suit feeds pages into the tray with the forbearance of someone long acquainted with the machine’s deficiencies."},{"k":"s","who":"Clare","t":"She does not turn. “First occasion seeing him.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I came for the pages.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Clare","t":"“Everyone comes for the pages. He holds a directorship. Eighteen, surname unverified, office on our floor. Allergic to small talk. Inclined to have you reassigned to a windowless office on the second floor should you attempt to engage him.” A single appraising glance, from the fractured heel of your shoe to your face. “Clare.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Clare","t":"“Scholars Program, I presume. I was you, two years and one jacket ago. So take the one thing I will offer you: the glass elevator sorts this building into the people who ride it and the people who watch. Do not let that sorting move into your skull. It is architecture. It is not destiny.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She squares her pages and goes."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"You need a director’s signature. The only director on this floor is the silver-haired boy who has never signed anyone’s anything. What is your move?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Approach him directly and introduce yourself."},{"key":"B","t":"Engineer a reason to be assigned to his project."},{"key":"C","t":"Find a more approachable director on another floor."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He does not slow his stride. “Who is this? Have her removed.”"},{"k":"n","t":"By Monday your desk has been relocated to a windowless office on the second floor, and the Scholars deadline passes unsigned while your colleagues avoid your name as though it were an infection carried by ambition."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You do not follow him. You return to your desk, open the internal archive, and begin searching for every mention of Northridge that has crossed the sixth floor in the last quarter."},{"k":"s","who":"Ethan","t":"Without turning from his screen, he says, “That is, unfortunately, the first intelligent thing you have done this morning.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You write down three names from other floors, each more approachable than the last, and by Wednesday each assistant has returned the same soft refusal in a different font."},{"k":"n","t":"No director outside this floor knows your work well enough to risk their name on it, and the nomination window closes unsigned."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"In the lobby, brushed-steel capitals impress the firm’s name into dove-grey marble. Beneath them, in a diminished register: Established 1987. Integrity. Precision. Silence."},{"k":"n","t":"You take out the notepad. On its final page, in the smallest hand you can execute:"},{"k":"n","t":"Director. Northridge. Forty-eight hours."}]},{"n":2,"title":"The Price of a Seat","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Canvas occupies the ground floor of a converted tailor’s premises in Respite Quarter, wedged between a locksmith whose window display has not been disturbed since the present decade commenced and a photographic studio whose proprietor long ago abandoned the pretence that a new tenant might materialise. The espresso machine is a temperamental instrument that surrenders its steam only to the palm that has learned the exact coordinate at which to strike its flank. You have learned it. It is, on most afternoons, the single thing in the establishment that obeys you."},{"k":"n","t":"At the window table, the most conspicuous in the room, four girls have installed themselves around an arrangement of four drinks and one untouched pistachio cake, and the conversation they are conducting is the kind that is performed rather than held, pitched half a register above necessity for the benefit of anyone within range who might wish to know how little they are wanted in it."},{"k":"s","who":"Rowan","t":"She rotates an earring set with a pearl older than her family’s money. “He declined the Echelon City Economic Forum. Full press, full alumni board, a fee most faculty would not see in a decade. Declined it. And now he consents to deliver the keynote at a student dinner chaired by an eighteen-year-old whose grandmother bought her the chair. One does wonder what the calculation was.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"She turns her cortado a precise quarter-turn upon its saucer, the gesture of a woman who disagrees and has not yet decided how expensively to do so. “There is no calculation. Hosea Su does what interests him and declines what does not, and the entire city has spent three years mistaking his boredom for strategy. The forum bored him. Apparently the dinner does not. I would not read scripture into a seating chart.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Pearl","t":"“They are saying the table nearest the lectern is already gone. That people are trading favours for a chair from which he can be seen.” She lowers her voice into the shape of reverence. “Tessa Vaughn offered her family’s box at the symphony for a single seat in his eyeline. He has not so much as confirmed he will look at the room.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“He will look at the room. He always looks at the room. It is the one democratic thing about him: he distributes a glance to every quadrant and withholds himself from all of them equally.” The cup completes its turn. “The mistake every girl at this table makes is believing the glance was aimed. It is weather, Pearl. You do not get rescheduled for being rained on.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Behind the counter, you load a tray. Across the back of Vivienne’s cream skirt, undetected by its wearer and catalogued already by the three faces arranged around her, a stain has announced itself in the hour since she last stood."},{"k":"n","t":"Rowan’s phone is in her lap. It has been in her lap for some minutes, angled, its lens describing a discreet arc each time its owner is not addressed directly."},{"k":"n","t":"You carry the cake to the table. Beneath the doily, flat against the ceramic, sealed and slim, sits a thing the situation requires and no one at the table will offer her."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Vivienne’s humiliation is one motion away in three directions, and only one of them leaves her standing. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Slip the pad beneath the doily and say nothing."},{"key":"B","t":"Tell her quietly before the others can act."},{"key":"C","t":"Serve the cake and stay out of it."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Vivienne lifts the doily a half-inch, registers what is beneath it, and her face performs nothing whatever. Her hand drifts to the small of her back, and the cardigan comes off her shoulders and knots at her waist in a motion indistinguishable from idleness."},{"k":"s","who":"Rowan","t":"Her phone remains angled, uselessly, at a humiliation that has already failed to occur."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"She thanks you with a smile cold enough to preserve meat. “How thoughtful.”"},{"k":"n","t":"By the weekend Canvas has lost its richest table, and Mira is told, with perfect politeness, that certain establishments are better at discretion than others."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Rowan’s lens completes its arc. Vivienne stands, and the room receives the thing it had been waiting to watch."},{"k":"n","t":"Three days later, the vacant seat at Chancellor’s Garden is offered to a girl who did not decide safety was the same thing as intelligence."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The pistachio is cut, eaten, set down. Then Vivienne, in a voice pitched for the room rather than the table, dismantles the afternoon."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“Pearl has a resubmission with Professor Dao because I requested it. Rowan has a summer placement because I drafted the letter her mother could not. So one of you will, in the next five seconds, tell me what the three of you have been swallowing since I returned from the restroom.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Pearl","t":"The betrayal leaves her before her conscience can intercept it. “Rowan was filming you. From under the table. When you walked back.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The flat white is lifted, held above the offending crown, and inverted with the unhurried deliberation of a woman who has resolved to stop being managed. Milk descends through inherited pearls and into the collar of an ironed blouse, and the café performs the particular silence of a room that has understood, all at once, that it should not have been watching."},{"k":"n","t":"Vivienne deletes three photographs from a phone surrendered without resistance, returns it face-down, and turns to the counter as though nothing requiring her attention has occurred until this precise moment."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“Your name.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Sealyra. Xie.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“I have one vacant seat for Saturday. Chancellor’s Garden, Tenure Hill, two o’clock. Hosea Su delivers the keynote, an eighteen-year-old pretends a foundation chair is a coronation, and I find I would sooner spend the afternoon beside a waitress with a functioning mind than another girl who mistakes cowardice for manners.”"},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"The seat is a door. It is also, plainly, a leash with a longer chain than usual. How do you take it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Accept with gratitude."},{"key":"B","t":"Ask what she expects in return."},{"key":"C","t":"Call it what it sounds like: a punishment seat."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Vivienne","delta":-5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Thank you. I’ll come.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"Her eyes cool by a degree. “How sweet.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She takes the card back between two fingers, writes the address anyway, and leaves you with the uncomfortable knowledge that you have entered as a recipient rather than a guest."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Vivienne","delta":3},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“And what is it you’ll want from me.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"She sets her card down with something between approval and contempt. “There it is. I offer a seat and you invoice me for the terms. We will get along, Xie, which is not the same as my liking you.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Vivienne","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“That sounds like a punishment seat.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Vivienne pauses, and then smiles with something her face has not rehearsed."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“It is a seat at Paragon. Punishment is included in the tuition. Two o’clock. Wear black if you own it.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The bell over the door rings her out into the afternoon. Behind the machine, Mira polishes a cup that does not require it."},{"k":"s","who":"Mira","t":"She does not look up. “That table loses me money. I keep it because girls from Tenure Hill make this street look safer than it is, and because once a year one of them does something worth watching.” A glance, finally, at you. “You should not have gone near it. You went near it anyway. Wear shoes you can run in on Saturday.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You take out the notepad. Beneath the line about Northridge, a second:"},{"k":"n","t":"Saturday. Tenure Hill. Watch the one everyone watches."}]},{"n":3,"title":"The Weight of a Scar","lead":"jael","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"St. Remedy Neurological Institute terminates a cul-de-sac flanked by paulownia trees, pale stone and smoked glass that, after the hour at which visitors are discouraged, resembles less a hospital than a vessel left running after its crew has gone ashore. On the eighth floor the corridor lighting is dimmed past ten into a register that the administration considers restful and that you have come, across months of arriving at precisely this hour, to associate with the specific exhaustion of a place that never closes and never improves."},{"k":"n","t":"Room 812 contains a boy whose hair has outgrown the kitchen-scissor correction you administered to it last month, growing with the indifferent persistence of a body that has not been informed its occupant is elsewhere."},{"k":"n","t":"The visitor’s chair is occupied when the corridor light spills across the floor ahead of you."},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"He rises with the unhurried economy of a man who has never in his life been rushed by a room he entered. “Jael Wen. Aubade Family Office. The foundation administers the research grants for the clinical-trial programme, and I am here on the sponsor’s behalf. Forgive the hour. I find the people worth meeting are rarely available during the ones the institution prefers.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Sit, then. Tell me what you are selling and what it costs.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"“Nothing is for sale. That is the difficulty people have with it.” The folder opens upon his knee with a deliberateness that suggests every page has been ordered to manage exactly how much a frightened person can absorb. “A flexible implant. A bridge laid across the pathways the injury severed. The brain, in the most favourable outcome, relearning the practice of attending to its own body. I will not use the word awakening, because the word does work the data has not earned.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The cadence is calibrated. Each sentence concludes a beat before you can mount an objection to it, so that agreement arrives feeling like your own idea. You have met salesmen. You have not met one who declines to appear to be selling, and the refusal is itself the most accomplished sale you have witnessed."},{"k":"n","t":"Then, when the folder is closed and the gravity has been established, the register changes into something warmer, lighter, almost incidental."},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"“It is a great deal to carry on your own. Forgive me, but I have read the intake file, and there is no second name on it. No parents listed. No other relations who visit.” A pause weighted to feel like sympathy. “Who else knows the situation is as serious as it is? Who helps you carry it?”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"The question is upholstered in concern. The man asking it works for a sponsor you have never met. How do you answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Tell him the truth: no one."},{"key":"B","t":"Keep the answer clinical."},{"key":"C","t":"Ask why he wants to know."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"“No one at all,” he repeats, softly, and writes nothing down, which is worse than if he had."},{"k":"n","t":"By the time he leaves, the room has not changed, the monitor has not changed, and the danger has not yet taken a shape you can point to; all that has happened is that a stranger’s employer now knows you are movable."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The foundation has the scans, the prognosis, and the consent history. The composition of my family is not a variable in the trial.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"He inclines his head, and the warmth withdraws so politely that its absence almost looks like respect. “Of course. I overstepped.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Why is the emotional architecture of my household on a clinical file.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"For a half-second something genuine crosses his face, and it is not embarrassment. “It is not.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He leaves you his card anyway, but on Wednesday the trial packet is delayed, the bed transfer is “under review,” and no one will tell you which sentence made the room colder."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"A card is produced, heavier than ordinary stock, and held across the bed until your fingers rise to claim it. “Wednesday. The full packet, the failure rates, the withdrawal terms, all of it. Read every line. I would think less of you if you signed without.” He pauses at the door. “He has your jaw, your brother. People who have a sibling tend not to notice. People who have lost one notice immediately.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He is gone before you can decide whether the last sentence was sympathy or reconnaissance."},{"k":"n","t":"You sit. Your thumb finds, without instruction, the smoothed tissue along the underside of your brother’s jaw, and the room recedes, and the year recedes, and you are thirteen again in an apartment that smelled of liquor and the cold coming through a door someone had left open."},{"k":"n","t":"You were teaching him English from a textbook salvaged from a recycling bin. He was seven. He was drawing, on the back of an electricity bill, a horse with six legs, the surplus pair reserved, by his own testimony, for emergencies."},{"k":"n","t":"The footsteps that crossed the living room were uneven, which meant drinking, and heavy, which meant losing."},{"k":"n","t":"The open hand fell against the side of your face. Once. Then again."},{"k":"s","who":"Kye","t":"He rose from the floor. Seven years old, arms extended from his sides in the posture of a child who has seen, on a screen or in a book, a smaller body placed between a bad thing and a good one, and resolved, without the faintest comprehension of the mechanics, to attempt it. The only English word he owned, harvested from the first page of that salvaged textbook, was the one printed in red beneath a picture of a raised hand. “Don’t.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The arm that swept him aside deposited him against the cabinet whose hinge had stood open since the previous winter, and the corner of that hinge took the underside of his jaw, and the sound it made was brief and wet and is the loudest thing you have ever heard."},{"k":"n","t":"Outside the clinic afterward, a sock on one foot and none on the other, his blood already through the gauze, he asked you whether it would scar."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“It will. But a scar only means something happened, and that you are still here.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The room reassembles in monitor-blue and lamp-gold. The ventilator keeps its patient rhythm. Your thumb has not left the scar."},{"k":"n","t":"You have read the foundation’s reputation, audited what can be audited, and found, where a catch should be, only a silence you do not trust and cannot disprove. The packet will arrive Wednesday. You already know what your hand will do when it does."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I am going to sign it. Because the only ending I am capable of wanting is the one where you come back. Even if I cannot yet see who I am borrowing it from.”"}]},{"n":4,"title":"The Forum","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The Hargrove auditorium seats four hundred beneath a ceiling of stone and glass, and from the fifth row, into which Vivienne has installed the two of you with the proprietary ease of a woman who has never sat further back, the stage is close enough that the speakers will address you whether they mean to or not."},{"k":"n","t":"The Dean introduces a third-year economist whose papers have travelled further than their author’s modesty admits, and the room rearranges its faces before the name is finished."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He attains the lectern in the manner of a person so habitually awaited that arrival has stopped costing him anything, and he sets one hand on the wood without troubling to adjust a microphone that someone has already set to his height. “My subject is how industrial output, the morbidity of the men who produce it, and the poisoning of the water that takes their waste are not three problems but one mechanism wearing three faces, and how a regulator financed by the very firms it inspects has been, in plain language, purchased by the people it was built to restrain.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The applause arrives and he has already moved past it, which you note, because a man who waits for applause is a man who needs it, and this one appears to need nothing the room can supply."},{"k":"s","who":"Dean","t":"“Our second speaker requires, I think, no introduction, though he has consented to so few of these in his career that the faculty considers tonight something of an occasion. He has never before addressed this forum. That he does so now is, I am told, a first, and we are conscious of the privilege.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He crosses from the right wing, silver-haired and eighteen, adjusts the microphone down by exactly one inch, and declines to acknowledge the privilege the Dean has just pressed upon the room. “The orthodoxy I intend to interrogate is the belief that frontier artificial intelligence requires three irreducible inputs: enormous datasets, enormous infrastructure, and an enormous expenditure of energy. The belief is empirically untenable. Innovation under constraint discovers what innovation under abundance never troubles to look for, and the firms that spent a decade buying their dominance are about to learn that engineering cannot be purchased at the exchange rate of hardware.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The applause this time is briefer and less certain, the sound of a room unsure whether it has witnessed a prodigy or a very expensive adolescent, and he acknowledges none of it."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Shiro’s argument turns on one central claim. What is it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Read his point as compute and capital scaling performance."},{"key":"B","t":"Read his point as constraint forcing better engineering."},{"key":"C","t":"Read his point as energy cost being AI’s main barrier."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“So your point is that performance scales with compute and capital—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He looks at you just long enough to register that you absorbed nothing. “If that is what you heard, you were not listening.”"}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Your point is that constraint forces better engineering than abundance ever does.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"A pause. “Adequate.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He moves on, but not as quickly from you as from the rest."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“So the real barrier is the energy cost—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Then you misheard the talk entirely.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He is already turning away."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"As Hosea, seated now among the faculty, lets his gaze travel the hall, it pauses on the fifth row, on a seat that is one to Vivienne’s left, for a beat longer than the rest of the arc requires, before it moves on."},{"k":"n","t":"The speeches are over and the hall is dissolving into the part of the evening where careers are made by who approaches whom."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Approach Hosea."},{"key":"B","t":"Approach Shiro."},{"key":"C","t":"Leave with Vivienne."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Vivienne","delta":-10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"Her face gets there before her mouth. “You’re walking up to Hosea Su? A waitress in a borrowed blazer, in front of this faculty?”"},{"k":"n","t":"The laugh is not kind."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“A toad reaching for the sky.”"},{"k":"n","t":"By the corridor she has blocked you and made three calls. Tenure Hill files your name under delusional before the evening is over."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"[Shiro −10] [Vivienne +10]"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Mr. Si—I wanted to ask whether the reinforcement loop scales beyond language—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I do not know you.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Three words, flat, and he is gone before your second clause."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"Instead of contempt, something almost fond crosses her face. “You went for the cold one. Clinically deranged about a boy who looked through you like glass, and somehow the first interesting thing you’ve done all day.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She loops her arm through yours."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You reach for no one, and the hall empties around you with all its possible beginnings intact and unused."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“Boys assembled like that don’t know girls assembled like you. Apply for the degree, Sealyra. Not the boy.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’m going to apply. Computer Science. For the degree.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"Gerald brings the car around, and in the leather dark of the back seat Vivienne watches the campus fall behind."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“The degree will outlast him. The boy will not retain your face by Tuesday.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You watch the lit windows of Concord recede, and you do the arithmetic that has nothing to do with any boy in that hall, because the thing you want from this place was never a person."}]},{"n":5,"title":"The Signature","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The chair facing the senior associate’s desk is leather, and you lower yourself into it at half past eight while the supervisor, fifteen years in the firm’s employ and never once late by a margin exceeding sixty seconds, reads your report without yet conceding that you are in the room."},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Section one. Northridge confronts an impending recession in its principal domestic market, and has responded by retrenching operations, downsizing its manufacturing base, and refinancing its outstanding debt through subordinated bonds issued at a diminished coupon, which is the correct sequence of moves and the incorrect sequence of sentences in which you have described them.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She turns a page without looking up."},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Demand has begun to dwindle, margins are anticipated to erode, and the payroll has been pared by a figure the management euphemistically designates redundancy and the discharged workforce designates by its proper name, which you have reproduced verbatim from their press release as though a press release were a source rather than a confession.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“You have also written, of the national operating deficit, that it has gone up, and that phrasing is the reason we are going to have a conversation about whether your English is fit to leave this floor under my name.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The red pen lifts from the page and hangs there, a hawk declining to commit to the descent."},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Correct the verb. A deficit does not go up. Tell me what it does.”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"The supervisor is not asking rhetorically, and the Scholars nomination she has not yet agreed to sign is sitting somewhere behind this answer. What does a deficit do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Say the deficit has increased."},{"key":"B","t":"Say the deficit has widened."},{"key":"C","t":"Say the deficit has grown larger."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The deficit has increased.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Increased is not wrong, which is the most damning thing I can say about it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She closes the folder on your nomination before it opens."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The deficit has widened.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"She makes the mark, and the mark is the closest thing to praise this office dispenses. “Widened. Fits the register, the field, and the fact.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The deficit has grown larger.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“That is the English of a person describing a balloon, not a balance sheet.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The nomination is withdrawn before it is filed."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Section two is the weakest of the three, because you wrote that entry into the British market will produce revenue, which is a sentence containing the appearance of information and none of its substance: revenue denominated in which currency, net of which tariff, adjusted for which exchange-rate volatility, and hedged through which instrument, because a figure rendered in sterling this morning is a materially altered figure by tomorrow’s close if the merchant moving the goods has not locked the exposure through a currency swap with a willing counterparty.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She closes the folder at last, and folds her hands upon it."},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Section three, the auction, is the only competent thing in the binder, and it is competent because you described what you understood rather than what you hoped, which is a discipline I would like you to extend retroactively to the first two sections by Wednesday. Was there something else you wanted, or did you intend to keep looking at me as though there were.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The Scholars nomination. The form requires a director-level endorsement, and your authority as Mr. Si’s internal representative qualifies you to walk one in without a separate directorial review.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“I will consider it, which is a different verb from agree, and I would advise you to attend to the distinction, because if I attach my credibility to a junior clerk who then fails the entrance examination, the failure is attributed to my judgement as readily as to hers, and a senior associate who endorses a poor candidate forfeits standing on this floor for two fiscal years that I am not inclined to spend repairing.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She uncaps the pen again."},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Go. I have a compliance audit and you have two sections to rebuild.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The corridor outside the investment director’s suite admits two occupants and no third witness on the Thursday you have engineered to be there, and the director emerges with a leather portfolio beneath one arm and the particular indifference of a person who has decided the corridor is empty regardless of who is standing in it."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"He has, on three prior occasions, declined to know you. You have questions about the report and a debt you are tempted to acknowledge. How do you open?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Stop him and raise the report directly."},{"key":"B","t":"Let him pass and use Margolis’s formal route."},{"key":"C","t":"Stop him and thank him."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Mr. Si—about the three sections, I think—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Watch yourself, miss. If you intend to keep your seat in this building.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The elevator takes him down, and by afternoon Margolis knows you tried to move around her. The nomination does not survive your cleverness."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You let him pass. The file goes through Margolis, the outer desk, the assistant, and reaches the right person with no witness to your hunger."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Mr. Si—thank you. For the things you didn’t have to do.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He stops. “For what.”"},{"k":"n","t":"By afternoon a compliance officer is asking why a junior clerk is soliciting a director off the record. No answer keeps you employed."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The envelope arrives on your keyboard the following Friday without a stamp, and inside it a single sheet bearing a completed nomination form and, at its foot, a signature small and angular and executed by a hand that declines to squander ink upon curves."},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“He signed it himself, which I confirmed with his assistant before you ask, and no, I do not know on what basis, because the man does not explain his decisions to the people they benefit.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She does not look up from her audit."},{"k":"s","who":"Margolis","t":"“Understand what you are holding. He does not give compliments, he does not give encouragement, he gives corrections, and the quantity of red ink he spends on a document is the only measure he offers of how closely he read it. Your report bled. He read every word of it twice and signed his name beneath yours regardless, which is more than this floor has extracted from him in two years, and I would caution you against deciding what it means, because the last person who decided what his attention meant is no longer employed in this hemisphere.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You take the form with both hands."},{"k":"n","t":"On the bus home, under light the colour of old coins, you write one more line in the notepad."},{"k":"n","t":"Concord. Nomination secured. Do not waste it."}]},{"n":6,"title":"The Direction Reverses","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The examination hall smells of paper, polished wood, and the particular panic of adults who have staked their futures on a reading passage about educational policy. The final section is an argument about scholarship schemes: the author criticises the way elite institutions frame access as generosity, notes that financial support alone cannot dismantle inherited advantage, and refuses, with irritating precision, to say whether such schemes are effective or ineffective overall."},{"k":"n","t":"The question beneath it asks:"},{"k":"n","t":"Scholarship programmes successfully eliminate structural inequality in higher education."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"What is the correct answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Yes."},{"key":"B","t":"No."},{"key":"C","t":"Not Given."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You mark Yes."},{"k":"n","t":"The invigilator collects the paper, and the answer stays behind in your head like a bruise: the author criticised the framing, not confirmed the outcome. When the list posts, your name is not on it."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You mark No."},{"k":"n","t":"The author never ruled scholarship programmes ineffective; you supplied a conclusion the passage refused to give, and the lost point becomes the one that keeps your name below the line."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You mark Not Given."},{"k":"n","t":"The author critiques the premise without issuing a verdict, and the distinction is thin enough to punish the careless and clear enough to save the prepared."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The result resolves on the portal at the corner table in Canvas, where Mira has set down a flat white you did not order and then found something to wipe at the far end of the bar, because she is the kind of person who knows when a girl needs a screen read in private."},{"k":"n","t":"The line says you are in."},{"k":"n","t":"You read it three times before you trust it, and the third time your hand is not entirely steady on the cup."},{"k":"n","t":"The phone lights on the bus home, with a name you have been declining to think about."},{"k":"s","who":"Jenny","t":"“How did your little exam go? Tell me honestly, I won’t laugh. Share my news first, though, because mine is real: Brookline Law. After all these years. Some of us get there the long way.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Congratulations, Jenny.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Jenny","t":"“You don’t sound like a person who got good news today. Did you even sit it? The Concord thing? I only ask because the acceptance rate on that track is, what, a rounding error. I’d hate for you to have got your hopes up over something that was never going to—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I got in.”"},{"k":"n","t":"There is a silence on the line of exactly the length a person needs to rearrange every sentence they had loaded for the call."},{"k":"s","who":"Jenny","t":"“In where.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Concord. The honours track. This morning.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Jenny","t":"“You’re joking. That’s—they don’t just—”"},{"k":"n","t":"Then nothing, because there is nothing in the script she rehearsed that survives contact with the fact, and the call ends without her finding the end of the sentence."},{"k":"n","t":"You put the phone face-down in your lap and watch the city go by, and you do not smile, exactly, but something in your shoulders comes down."},{"k":"n","t":"The dormitory for the supplemental-admission freshmen is an old brick building that smells of floor wax and radiator dust, and Room 207 is at the end of the second-floor corridor with one bunk already half unpacked when you arrive."},{"k":"n","t":"The girl kneeling by the lower bed looks up, and her face does the thing few faces in this city have done at the sight of you, which is to simply be glad of company."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“Oh—hi. You must be the fourth. I’m Veda.” She is already on her feet, already reaching to take one end of your bag without being asked. “Here, the corner by the window’s yours. It gets the morning sun, so I left it for whoever came last because the other two wanted the inside beds. Have you eaten? I have biscuits. They’re not nice biscuits, but they’re biscuits.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Sealyra. And no. Thank you.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“My family’s three hours out, near Cargill. Bus driver and a supermarket till between them, so.” She says it plainly, without apology, the way you say a thing to someone you have correctly guessed will not look down on you for it. “You don’t have to tell me yours. I just figured I’d say mine first so it’s even.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You like her before you have decided to, which is rare enough that you notice it."},{"k":"n","t":"The door opens without a knock, and the temperature of the room changes."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"Two girls enter mid-conversation, the taller one trailing a scent that costs more than your month, and she takes you in with a glance that begins at your bag and does not bother to finish. “Oh. They did put a fourth in. I suppose they had to. Wren, this is—sorry, what was it?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Wren","t":"“She’s the supplemental one.” Wren says it from half a step behind Thea, in the voice of a person borrowing someone else’s authority to be unkind. “The charity place. Off the assistant track or something. There was a thing about it.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“Mm. Well.” Thea sets a cream leather case on the inside bed that has clearly been claimed in advance. “It’s only that this room was meant to be the three of us, originally, and now it’s a little cosier than planned. No one’s fault. We’ll all just have to be considerate about space.” The smile she gives you is the smile of a door closing politely in your face."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"She has drawn the line in front of everyone and is waiting to see what you do with it. You will live in this room for a year. How do you answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Push back and claim your place."},{"key":"B","t":"Give her an exit and yield the space."},{"key":"C","t":"Compliment her enough to be tolerated."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Thea","delta":-10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I tested in. You were assigned a bed. One of those took work.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"Her face closes. “How nice for you.”"},{"k":"n","t":"By the second week no one sits with you. Being right is the most expensive thing a girl with no money can buy."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The room’s smaller than any of us wanted. Take the inside beds and the wardrobe by the door. I keep odd hours anyway.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"She takes the exit, because she cannot fight someone who refuses to hold the other end of the rope."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Thea","delta":-5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“That case is gorgeous—is it this season?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"Her smile warms by a degree. “You have an eye, at least.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She files you as someone a compliment can buy, which is a worse shelf than poor."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"When the two of them go out to find dinner somewhere you were conspicuously not invited, Veda exhales as though she has been holding it."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“You handled that better than I did on my first day. I went red and said nothing for an hour.” She passes you a biscuit after all. “Thea’s not cruel, exactly. She just genuinely cannot see people like us, the way you can’t see through a window you’ve never been on the other side of. Wren’s worse, because Wren can see us; she just decided being Thea’s shadow pays better.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Thea has the inside bed and the better wardrobe and she still made it sound like we crowded her.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“She’s distracted, to be fair to her, which I am trying to be. There’s a boy—Rhys Tang, he rows with Hosea Su, talks more than three people put together—he’s been chasing her since the summer. Flowers to the wrong dorm, the whole thing.” She lowers her voice with the pleasure of someone sharing harmless gossip with a friend for the first time in a while. “Thea lets him, because being chased by one of Hosea’s friends keeps her one handshake from Hosea, and Hosea is the one she actually wants. Everybody knows. Nobody says.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The name settles somewhere in the back of your mind, in the place you keep things that are not useful yet."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“Anyway. Orientation’s tomorrow, nine o’clock, the big hall. We can walk over together, if you want. It’s nicer than going in alone.” She says it carefully, the way you offer something you are half braced to have refused."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’d like that.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Her whole face changes, and you realise she had expected a no, and you file that too: that kindness, in a place like this, has learned to flinch before it is even answered."}]},{"n":7,"title":"The One Everyone Watches","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Orientation morning, and the four of you leave Room 207 in the formation that will define the year: Thea and Wren ahead, walking the half-step in front that says they are not quite with you, and Veda beside you, matching your pace without being asked."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“You slept badly.” She says it gently, not as an accusation. “I heard you up at four. If it’s the bed, we can swap. Mine’s further from the radiator; it knocks all night.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“It’s not the bed. I’m not used to having somewhere I’m allowed to stay.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She doesn’t fill the silence after that with anything stupid, which is its own kind of kindness, and you walk the rest of the way to the hall in a quiet that does not need managing."},{"k":"n","t":"The auditorium is the same one that held the autumn forum, and the freshman class pours into it in their hundreds, and the seats near the front have already been colonised by the students who arrived knowing they belonged at the front. Thea steers Wren toward them. Veda steers the two of you toward the middle, where people like you are neither noticed nor in anyone’s way."},{"k":"n","t":"The Dean speaks. Then the lights settle, and the name that crosses the room before the introduction is finished is one the whole front section pronounces under its breath like a password."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He crosses to the lectern, and whatever you remembered from the forum has, in the weeks since, sanded itself down into something you could dismiss; in person it refuses to be dismissed. He does not perform. He simply occupies the front of a room of four hundred strangers as though the room had been waiting for him to arrive and complete it. “You have spent twelve years being read by other people—by teachers, by examiners, by the machines that scored you. From this morning the direction reverses. The university is not here to read you. It is here to teach you how to read: the books, yes, but also the room, the silence in a seminar, the question that was asked and the one that was carefully avoided. Intellectual humility is not the opposite of confidence. It is the thing confidence is built on.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Around you the hall has gone still in the particular way a room goes still for someone it has decided to admire, and you feel it work on you too, the pull of it, and you note, because you cannot switch the noting off, that the warmth reaching you from the lectern is too even, too perfectly distributed, to be accidental. It is a warmth that has been engineered. Which does not make it less warm. It makes it more interesting."},{"k":"s","who":"Wren","t":"She has turned half around in her seat, whispering hot and fast. “He looked over here. Thea, he looked over—he knows your family, doesn’t he, didn’t your mothers—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“We summered at the same place on the coast when we were children. It’s not a thing one announces.” She announces it. “Rhys keeps trying to arrange a dinner, the four of us, but I won’t have Hosea thinking I needed Rhys to engineer it.” The smile she wears is for the benefit of the row behind, who are listening, who were meant to listen. “Some connections don’t require staging.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"She leans close, her voice for you alone, and there is no malice in it, only the quiet accuracy of someone who has spent a year watching from the cheap seats. “The summer-house thing is true, but they were eight, and Hosea has summered with half the families in this hall. Thea’s not lying. She’s just arranging the facts to stand at their most flattering angle. Everyone here does it about him. It’s like a currency. Knowing him, having stood near him, having been looked at—people spend it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"And then, as Hosea’s gaze completes its slow even arc of the hall, it reaches your row, and it pauses, not on Thea two rows ahead, not on the front where it was awaited, but on the middle, on you, for a beat longer than the arc allows."},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"His attention has found you across four hundred people, and the girls who would kill for it are sitting closer than you are. What do you do with it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Hold his gaze."},{"key":"B","t":"Lower your eyes."},{"key":"C","t":"Let the glance pass."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You hold his eyes a breath too long. Something behind his composure recalibrates, because he gives that look to four hundred people a year, and almost none of them decline it."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":5},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You let your eyes fall at the speed of a girl who felt it land and chose not to want it."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":-5},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You give the pause nothing to catch on. It slides off you and finds the next row."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"When the address ends, the hall does not so much applaud as confirm something it already believed, and the front rows rise to cluster toward him while the middle files out toward the doors."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"She catches your sleeve at the aisle, which she has never once done, her eyes bright with a thing she has decided to make sound like generosity. “You’re in the cohort, so you’ll be at the mixers eventually. A word of advice, since no one will have given it to you: don’t be the girl who reads things into a glance. He looks at everyone. It means nothing, and it’s a little embarrassing to watch someone learn that the hard way.”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"She has dressed a warning as a kindness, in front of Wren, in front of the students still filing past. She wants to watch you absorb it. How do you take it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Accept the warning gratefully."},{"key":"B","t":"Pretend you noticed nothing."},{"key":"C","t":"Turn the warning back on her."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“You’re right. Thank you. I wouldn’t have known.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Thea glows at being deferred to, and files you as harmless."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Thea","delta":-10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“What glance?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"Her smile sharpens. “Don’t be clever, Sealyra. It looks desperate on people who cannot afford it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"By evening the room has learned that you enjoy making girls look foolish."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Thea","delta":-10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Of course. Though you did spend the morning telling everyone you summered with him.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"Her face closes. “How observant.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You meant it sweet. It landed as a blade. You sleep three feet from this girl."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"Outside, the autumn light is thin and clean across the quad, and Veda falls into step beside you with the unhurried loyalty of a person who has decided, without announcing it, whose side she is on."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“You let Thea win that one on purpose.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“She has the room, the group chats, and a year to make me miserable if she wants to. The glance cost me nothing to give away. Being right would have cost me everything.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“You think like someone who’s had to.” She says it softly, and then, because she is the kind of person who offers things before she’s sure they’ll be taken: “There’s a society fair on Friday. I was going to look at the naturalist one—the field trips, the expeditions. I don’t really have anyone to go with. You don’t have to. I just thought I’d ask before I lost my nerve.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The Naturalist Society. The expeditions. Somewhere at the edge of it, a name you have not yet been given a reason to learn."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Friday. I’ll come.”"}]},{"n":8,"title":"The Society Fair","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The society fair fills the cloisters on Friday with trestle tables and hand-lettered banners, and Veda steers the two of you through it with the particular focus of a person who has rehearsed where she is going and is pretending she has not."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“It’s that one. The Naturalist Society.” She nods toward a table at the far end, draped in field photographs: reed beds, a glacier, a bird you don’t recognise. “They do the expeditions. Actual fieldwork, real places. The patron funds it privately, so they go further than any other society can afford.” A pause, and then, quieter, the truth she has been walking toward: “The patron’s S. Si. He doesn’t come to things. But it’s his society. Being in it is the closest anyone gets.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You understand, then, where four in the morning’s worth of her courage has been going, and you say nothing, because some wants are kinder left unwitnessed."},{"k":"n","t":"You pass a piano-society table on the way: a quiet boy taking names, a recording playing low, a melody you half-recognise and cannot place, the kind of tune that seems to have been written for one listener and overheard by everyone else. It catches at something. You move on before it can."},{"k":"n","t":"At the Naturalist table, the banner names three patrons of the society’s various functions, and the third line stops you: a Student President whose name you have now heard pronounced like a password in two separate rooms."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He is there, behind the table, doing the unglamorous work of a society president on fair day, and when he looks up the warmth arrives half a beat before it is required, exactly as engineered. “You came to the autumn forum. Fifth row. You asked the cold one a question he didn’t deserve.” A faint smile. “I’m glad someone did. Are you signing up, or browsing?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Rhys","t":"A second boy materialises at Hosea’s shoulder before you can answer, all easy noise and unearned familiarity, a clipboard in one hand and his attention already half on a girl two tables over. “Sign her up, sign everyone up, we need names before the faculty cuts our budget. I’m Rhys, I do the talking, Hosea does the brooding, it’s a system.” He lowers his voice to a stage whisper that carries. “Is Thea coming to the fair? She said she might. Don’t tell her I asked.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"She has gone quiet beside you, a sign-up sheet half-filled, and you realise she is waiting to see whether putting her name down beside yours makes her braver or just more visible."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Hosea is waiting for your answer, in front of Rhys and a queue of freshmen who will remember how this goes. How do you sign up?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Ask what the society actually does."},{"key":"B","t":"Establish that you belong on merit."},{"key":"C","t":"Say you came with Veda."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’m here for the fieldwork. Before I sign, what does the society actually do?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He answers properly, because you asked a real question in a room asking none. “Field observation, conservation modelling, species indexing, and the occasional argument with a landowner who believes drainage is a personality.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Rhys","t":"“Finally. Someone who reads.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":-5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’m on the joint science track. I’m not here to admire the glacier.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"His smile holds, but cools. “No one asked whether you were.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The queue hears the insecurity before the merit."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":3},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“My friend wanted to come. I’d rather sign up with her.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Hosea’s gaze moves to Veda, almost approves, but this is not a room where loyalty qualifies you. The interview list posts without your name, and Veda apologises in a voice that makes the rejection worse."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"You write your name. Veda writes hers beneath it, close, the way you stand near someone in a crowd to remember you are not alone in it."},{"k":"s","who":"Rhys","t":"As you turn to go, he is still talking, to you, to the air, to anyone. “Selection starts next week, final cut in three. The patron does the last round himself, which never happens, because he never does anything himself unless it allows him to terrify people efficiently. He’ll ask you something that isn’t on any syllabus. He likes that. Drives the faculty mad.” He is already drifting toward the girl two tables over. “Tell Thea the dinner’s still on. If you see her. Which you will. You’re in her room, aren’t you—oh, that’s grim.”"},{"k":"n","t":"So the patron will be at the interview. The one who does not come to things. The one whose society is the closest anyone gets."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"The interview is your one chance in front of a man who controls the only expedition worth being on. How do you prepare?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Memorise the society’s published record."},{"key":"B","t":"Prepare to reason from what is in front of you."},{"key":"C","t":"Study the patron’s preferences."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Shiro asks the first question, and you answer with the winter expedition, the wetland report, the glacier photographs, and every published finding the society has archived."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“That was the syllabus.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He turns the page without looking at you again."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You prepare nothing but the instrument that has never failed you: reasoning from what is in front of you when you have never seen it before."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You walk in knowing what he funds, what he avoids, and what people say he hates."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“You studied me.”"},{"k":"n","t":"His voice makes it sound less like an accusation than a diagnosis."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"On the walk back she is lighter than you have seen her, her name on a list beside yours, a week between her and a building the patron might, conceivably, one day enter."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“I know it’s stupid. He doesn’t know I exist and he isn’t going to. But the society’s real, the fieldwork’s real, and I’d have signed up for it even if he’d never—”"},{"k":"n","t":"She stops, and decides to be honest with you, because you have not yet given her a reason not to be."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“It’s nice, having a thing that’s mine. Even if the reason I found it isn’t a thing I’d say out loud.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“You don’t have to say it out loud. I heard it anyway.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She laughs, surprised, and you walk back toward the dormitory in the thin gold light, two names on a list, one week closer to a room with the cold one in it."}]},{"n":9,"title":"The Trial","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The Naturalist Society was a piano society once. When Shiro Si bought it—funded it, renamed it, made it his—he turned it into something that goes into the field, and since the patron does not attend his own society, the running of it falls to the Student President, who handles intake the way he handles everything: in person, watchfully, with the warmth turned just high enough to be felt."},{"k":"n","t":"You are here for him. Not the fieldwork, not the glacier on the banner—him. The society is the door, and Hosea is the reason the door is worth opening."},{"k":"n","t":"He sits you down across a table scattered with field photographs."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“The places are real, the conditions are real, and people get hurt on real trips. So before I let anyone near a field site: three questions. Survival, not theory. Get one wrong and you don’t get in—and I’m told you can’t afford not to.” A faint smile. “Let’s see if you can keep yourself alive.”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Q1. Three days into a site, your water’s gone. Three sources in reach. Which do you drink?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"The fast stream running over rock."},{"key":"B","t":"The still, clear pond under the trees."},{"key":"C","t":"Rainwater pooled in a broad leaf."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The moving water—it’s running, so it’s cleaner.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“Cleaner than still. Not clean. There’s a dead animal upstream you can’t see, and by nightfall you can’t walk. There was a safer source on that list.”"}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The pond—I can see the bottom.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“Still water you can see the bottom of is a culture dish. Clear means nothing. You’d be on the ground by morning.”"}],"end":"END"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The rainwater—it hasn’t touched the ground or anything living.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"A small, real nod. “Rainwater, before it runs off. Correct. Most people reach for the stream and feel clever doing it.” Next."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Q2. A predator you can’t identify is on the trail ahead—large, eyes facing forward, holding its ground. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Turn and run for the treeline."},{"key":"B","t":"Stand tall, make yourself large, back away slowly without turning."},{"key":"C","t":"Drop flat and play dead."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Run—get to the trees.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“Forward-facing eyes mean a hunter, and you just turned your back on a hunter and ran. Now you’re prey. It’s faster than you. It was always faster than you.”"}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Make myself big, hold its eyes, back off slow—no turning, no running.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“You don’t run from a thing built to chase, and you don’t shrink in front of a thing that picks the weak. Correct.” Something flickers—he hadn’t expected the third one this clean. Last question."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Play dead—drop and stay still.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“That works on one kind of animal in one situation, and you don’t know which animal this is. You guessed, lying down, in front of a predator. It ate well.”"}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Q3. The fastest way back to camp crosses a dry riverbed. The sky is darkening upstream, over hills you can’t see past. Take it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Yes—it’s dry, flat, fastest before dark."},{"key":"B","t":"No—take the longer high route along the ridge."},{"key":"C","t":"Cross fast now, before the rain reaches you."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Take the riverbed—it’s dry and fastest.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“A dry riverbed under a darkening upstream sky is the most dangerous flat ground in the world. The rain’s already falling where you can’t see it. The wall of water reaches you before you hear it.”"}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“No. Rain upstream and a dry bed means flash flood. I take the high route and lose the time.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“Slow and alive over fast and underwater. That’s the whole job.” He sets the photographs down. “Three for three. I’ve watched people I liked fail the second one. Welcome to the society.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Cross now—beat the rain.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“The rain is already here, upstream. You walked into the riverbed to outrun a flood already coming down it.”"}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"You let the relief reach your shoulders. Hosea is reaching to sign you in, the field journals already half yours."},{"k":"n","t":"Then the door at the back of the room opens, the temperature drops, and a voice you were promised you would not have to deal with today says, flatly:"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Don’t sign anything yet.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He has come to his own society for the first time anyone present can remember, and he is looking directly at you, and he does not look like a man about to welcome you anywhere."}]},{"n":10,"title":"The Second Trial","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Shiro crosses the room without hurry, and the society—Hosea, the waiting candidates, the girl beside you—goes quiet around him the way a room goes quiet for someone it has been trained to fear."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He does not look at the field photographs. He looks at you. “She passed the survival questions. She also walked into Meridian, into the forum, into this society, each time arriving exactly where she meant to be. I don’t fund expeditions for people who plan their way into rooms. This society does not welcome the calculating.”"},{"k":"n","t":"It is aimed at you, in front of everyone, and it is unfair, and it is also—you understand with a cold drop—personal in a way you cannot account for, because the calculating is not a thing a stranger says about a girl he met three weeks ago."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He sets down his pen, unhurried, and does not raise his voice. “That’s a judgement, not a standard. If you’re going to keep someone out, keep them out by a rule that applies to everyone in this room—not by how much she unsettles you.” A beat. “Set a test. A real one. Let her fail it fairly or pass it fairly.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The patron and the president hold each other’s eyes for a moment, and something old and unspoken passes between them, and then Shiro turns to the piano against the far wall—the one furniture the society kept from what it used to be."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“This was a piano society before it was mine. So play. If you want in badly enough to plan your way here, you can play your way the rest of it. One piece. Convince the room you belong.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The candidates shift. Cassia—third-year, a Si-adjacent family, the girl whose face has been souring since you passed the survival round—sits at the keys first, plays something competent and forgettable, and steps back certain of herself."},{"k":"n","t":"Then it is you. You can play. You have always been able to play. And you have a choice about what."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Shiro has made the second trial a piano test. What do you play?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Play luvpoem—the one piece your fingers know cold, the one that lives in you, written for you a long time ago by a name you deleted."},{"key":"B","t":"Play the hardest thing in your repertoire—a virtuoso piece, all technical fire, built to make a room sit up."},{"key":"C","t":"Play something small and safe—competent, unshowy, nothing that risks embarrassment."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Your hands find luvpoem before you have decided to let them, and it pours out of you, quiet and aching and entirely too intimate for a room full of strangers judging you."},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"“What even is that? I’ve never heard it. It’s barely a piece—my little cousin plays harder than that.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The room agrees with her. It is too simple, too small, too unknown to impress anyone who came to be impressed, and you have spent your one chance on the one song that meant everything to you and nothing to them. You do not get in."},{"k":"n","t":"At the piano’s edge, the patron’s face does something you are too distracted to catch."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You choose the hardest thing you own and you do not hold back—a torrent of technical fire, both hands flying, the kind of piece that makes a room forget to breathe. When you lift your hands the silence is the good kind."},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"Her face has curdled completely."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He says nothing for a moment. Then, flatly, to Hosea: “Sign her in.” He does not look pleased. He looks, if anything, like a man who set a trap and watched the wrong person walk out of it intact."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You play something small and clean, technically fine, deliberately unremarkable—and unremarkable is exactly what the room remembers."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“She played not to lose. I asked her to convince the room she belonged. She did neither.” He turns away. “Next.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You hid your hand so well that no one saw there was anything in it."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"Hosea signs you in, and the matter is, officially, closed."},{"k":"n","t":"It does not stay closed."},{"k":"n","t":"By the next morning Cassia is in the society too—her name appears on the roster without explanation, and the explanation is the one everyone already knows: a family with ties to the Si name does not stay out of a Si society because she played badly. She is in. And she has been made, in front of the whole room, to look smaller than a supplemental-admission nobody who should not have been able to play at all."},{"k":"n","t":"She finds you in the corridor afterward, smile bright and eyes flat."},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"“You play beautifully. Truly. I’d never have guessed—you don’t look like someone who grew up with a piano.” A pause weighted to draw blood. “It’s just funny. The patron clearly can’t stand you, and somehow you keep ending up exactly where he is. People are going to wonder how a girl like you manages that.”"},{"k":"n","t":"She walks off before you can answer, and you file her—the way you file everything—and you do not yet know that this small humiliation, the one you handed her in front of the room, is the one she will spend the rest of the term repaying, and that the bill will come due on a yacht."}]},{"n":11,"title":"The Seat Beside Her","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The honours cohort is twelve people who arrived knowing each other’s families, and one supplemental-admission girl who arrived knowing the bus timetable. They do not bully you. Bullying would require acknowledging you exist. They simply close ranks—seats saved, group chats you are not in, lab pairings that leave you the odd one out—and let the silence do the work."},{"k":"n","t":"By the end of the first week you understand the arithmetic: alone, you are a target with no cover. You need to be inside something. And the only door into inside that this cohort respects runs through the girl who has the most to give and the most contempt to spend—Thea."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"The cohort is freezing you out. How do you survive it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Push back—stop letting them freeze you out, and make it cost them."},{"key":"B","t":"Get inside Thea’s orbit—make yourself useful to her, lower your head, and buy cover with the one currency you have."},{"key":"C","t":"Keep your head down alone and wait for it to pass."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“If you’ve got something to say about me, say it to my face instead of the group chat.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The cohort closes ranks harder. You have confirmed you are a problem, and a problem with no allies is a problem they can starve out. By midterm you eat alone, lab alone, and the isolation does what isolation does."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You find the thing Thea cannot do and you can—she is drowning in the statistics module, and you are not—and you offer it sideways, as deference rather than rescue, letting her keep the upper hand while you make yourself worth keeping around."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“You’re useful. I’ll say that much.” It is not friendship. It is cover, and cover is what you came for."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You keep your head down and wait, and the cohort takes the silence as permission, and the freezing-out hardens into the settled fact of your year. Nobody recruits the girl who sits alone and accepts it."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"Then, on a Tuesday, the impossible happens."},{"k":"n","t":"Shiro Si walks into the lecture hall."},{"k":"n","t":"The room stops. Shiro Si does not come to lectures—everyone knows it, it is one of the fixed facts of Concord, like the broken fountain. He is the patron who funds and never attends, the director who exists at a remove. And he is here, in a lecture hall, walking up the tiered seats, and he sits down in the empty seat beside you, sets out a notebook, and looks at the lecturer as though this is the most ordinary thing in the world."},{"k":"n","t":"The whispers start instantly. Why is he here. Why is he sitting there. Does he know her?"},{"k":"n","t":"What you notice, over the following days—because he comes back, every day, to the same seat—is that the cohort’s careful freezing-out of you has quietly thawed. No one saves the seat against you anymore. No one needs to, because the seat beside you is taken by the most feared person in the building, and no one is brave enough to treat you like a nobody while he is sitting there."},{"k":"n","t":"You do not understand it. He has never once spoken to you in the seat. He despises you, by his own public statement. And yet he is here, and his being here is, somehow, a wall at your back."},{"k":"n","t":"You decide, after a week of it, to risk the thing you actually need."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Shiro is sitting beside you every day, and you need help with the joint-track material. How do you ask?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Ask him straight—no excuse, no performance—if he will help you with the joint-track material."},{"key":"B","t":"Make your voice small and tell him how much you are struggling, hope he takes pity."},{"key":"C","t":"Offer him something—you will repay it, owe him, make it a transaction he cannot refuse."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“You’re the only person in this building who actually understands this material. I can’t afford a tutor and I can’t afford to fail. Will you help me or not.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He looks at you for a long moment, as though deciding whether the directness offends him or interests him. “…Sit up straight. You hold your pen like you’re afraid of it.” It is not a no. It is, somehow, a yes. He starts to explain, and within two sentences calls you a “spectacular waste of a cortex,” and within ten you have understood a thing you had been stuck on for a month."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’m really struggling, I don’t know what I’m doing, I thought maybe you could—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I don’t respond to helplessness. It’s a strategy, and a transparent one.” He returns to his notebook. The wall at your back stays, but the door you tried to open does not."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’ll pay you back somehow—I’ll owe you, whatever the rate is.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I am not a vending machine you put a debt into.” Flat, cold. “If you think the only reason a person helps you is because you’ve priced it, that tells me what kind of help you’ve had.” He does not offer any."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"So Shiro Si tutors you, in the seat beside you, in stolen ten-minute stretches after lectures, and the cohort cannot believe what it is seeing. The patron who attends nothing, who speaks to no one, who has never been observed doing a single thing that cost him effort, is explaining differential equations to the supplemental-admission girl and calling her a moron while he does it."},{"k":"s","who":"Cohort","t":"“He’s helping her—” “I’ve never seen him talk to anyone—” “Is she—are they—”"},{"k":"n","t":"They watch sideways, baffled, and rewrite their estimate of you a dozen times an hour, and not one of them lands on the truth, because the truth is not available to them: that the most guarded person in the building rearranged his entire schedule to sit beside a girl he claims to despise, and calls it nothing, and would die before he called it what it is."},{"k":"n","t":"You do not land on the truth either. You bank the tutoring—free, brilliant, exactly what you needed—and you read his rudeness as rudeness, and his presence as a useful accident, and his “spectacular waste of a cortex” as an insult rather than the only way he knows how to stay near you."},{"k":"n","t":"Then, one Friday, the seat beside you is empty. And the next day. And the one after."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“Si’s gone. Left campus—back to the company, they’re saying, some launch.” She watches your face. “You’ve got that look. The one where you’re doing arithmetic you don’t want to be doing.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’m not. He was a free tutor. Free tutors leave. That’s the whole transaction.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You tell yourself that, and you almost believe it, and you do not examine why the empty seat beside you is louder than the full one ever was."}]},{"n":12,"title":"The Wetland","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Shiro has been gone a week. The seat beside you in the lecture hall stays empty, and you have decided, with some effort, that it means nothing—free tutors leave, walls come down, and a girl who reads too much into an empty chair is a girl wasting attention she needs elsewhere. So you put the attention where it pays: on Hosea, on the society, on the wetland survey that the Student President is running himself this Saturday."},{"k":"n","t":"You did your homework for it the way you do everything—two nights ago, on a cracked phone screen at one in the morning, you watched the one film Veda mentioned that Hosea Su will discuss with anyone who’s earned the conversation. You came armed."},{"k":"n","t":"The reserve is forty minutes out, reed bed and shallow water, and the society fans out in pairs with clipboards. Hosea pairs himself with you. It is not an accident; he read the sheet before he handed it to anyone."},{"k":"n","t":"The clipboard fills—a heron exemplifying the patience of a specialist predator, a frog whose permeable skin makes it the first alarm a poisoned catchment ever sounds, amphibians failing long before the fish do. And then, at the water’s edge, Hosea sets it up."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“I watched something last week that works the way you do—refuses to tell you what anything is, only shows you what it does, and trusts you to assemble the rest.” He names the film. “You won’t have seen it. Almost no one has.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You have seen it. Two nights ago, for exactly this. And twenty feet away, Cassia—who has appointed herself to every society outing since you bested her, who is not in your class and does not know about the empty seat or the tutoring—has stopped pretending to photograph a reed."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“What stayed with you?”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"You watched it once, recently, deliberately, for exactly this moment. Cassia is twenty feet away pretending to photograph a reed. How do you answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Recount the scene everyone quotes, in precise detail—prove you watched it closely."},{"key":"B","t":"Name one small, unremarkable thing that lingered—the way a person remembers a feeling years later, not a plot."},{"key":"C","t":"Turn it back—ask what it meant to him, and stay safe behind the question."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The scene everyone quotes—the cut from the window to her hands, held three seconds too long—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"His smile holds, then cools. A person who saw a film long ago remembers a feeling; you remember the framing, because you watched it the night before last."},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"She has stopped photographing the reed. “Funny. I heard you tell someone last week you’d never even heard of it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The reserve goes quiet. You have been caught studying for a man, and a girl who studies for a man has every easy remark she has ever made turned suspect."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“There’s a man in the background of one shot. Never speaks, never matters—the only honest face in the whole film. I don’t know why he stayed with me, but he did.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He looks at you a moment longer than the answer needed. It is the kind of detail a plot summary cannot hold and a real viewing leaves behind—memory, not revision."},{"k":"n","t":"Cassia, straining to hear, gets nothing she can use."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":3},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“What did it mean to you?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He answers, because he likes to—but a small question lodges in him: why you had nothing of your own to say about a film you implied you knew."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The survey runs another hour. You log forty-some species to Hosea’s careful twenty, and the gap is not lost on him, and at the water’s edge where the light goes amber he stops."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“You’re the most prepared person in this society and you wear it like contraband—the film, the fieldwork, the way you knew exactly what to say and arranged your face to look like you didn’t.” Not an accusation. Closer to recognition. “I do the same thing. It’s lonely, being the only one in the room who already read it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"It is aimed with the precision you have learned to distrust, and it lands on something true anyway, and the two facts sit in you at once without cancelling—which is the exact discomfort he intends."},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"He has just told you that you are alike, and made the loneliness sound like intimacy. How do you answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Step into the mirror—agree you are the same kind of clever, take the alliance he is offering."},{"key":"B","t":"Refuse it—he hides it because he can afford to be forgiven; you hide it because you cannot. That is the whole difference."},{"key":"C","t":"Say nothing, and let the reeds and the amber light answer for you."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“It is lonely. Less so now, apparently.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"Pleased. A man who collects people he cannot predict has just watched one agree to be collected."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“You hide it because you can afford to be found out and forgiven. I hide it because I can’t. That’s the whole difference.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He goes quiet. You handed back a sharper version of yourself than the one he offered, and he finds it, to his inconvenience, more interesting."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":5},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You let the reeds answer. Hosea, who fills silences for a living, is unsure for once whether he won the exchange or conceded it."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"On the minibus back, Cassia migrates to the seat behind yours, sweet and probing."},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"“You and Hosea talked the whole survey. People will want to know what about.”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Cassia is gathering ammunition, and she will report whatever you give her. How do you handle her?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Tell her the truth—the fieldwork, a film he likes—and trust honesty to cover you."},{"key":"B","t":"Make it boring on purpose—species ratios, reed-encroachment data—until there is nothing worth carrying back."},{"key":"C","t":"Flip it—ask, warmly, what she made of the conservation work she came forty minutes for."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The fieldwork. And a film he likes.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"She carries the true version everywhere it can do damage: he paired himself with the new girl and discussed the one film he discusses with no one. It is more than enough."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Native-invasive ratios, mostly. He’s painfully thorough on the reed-encroachment figures.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"Her eyes glaze before you finish. Nothing to repeat, nothing to carry back. She gives up and faces front."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Cassia","delta":-10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“What did you make of the conservation side—you came the whole forty minutes for it?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"She watched the back of Hosea’s head for an hour and the wetland for none, and she has no answer, and she does not forgive being made to look foolish twice by the same girl."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"Veda drops into the seat beside you as the reeds blur past."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“You made the most interesting hour of your term sound like a soil report. I watched Cassia fall asleep with her eyes open.” A grin, then quieter. “You’ve got that look again, though. The arithmetic one. Something’s bothering you and it isn’t Cassia.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You think about the empty seat in the lecture hall. The week of it. The way you keep noticing it."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“It’s nothing. Someone left, that’s all. People leave.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“Mm.” She doesn’t push, which is its own kindness. “For what it’s worth, the patron sat next to you for two weeks and then vanished the day you stopped needing the tutoring. People who leave the second they’re not useful to you anymore are easy. People who leave the second they did something for you—” she shrugs, watching the window “—those ones I’d keep an eye on.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You do not answer. But the arithmetic, the kind you do not want to be doing, has already started."}]},{"n":13,"title":"The Bridge","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You have spent three weeks making yourself useful to Thea Song. Not friendly—useful. You catch her statistics errors before she submits them, you remember which faculty she is trying to impress, you hand her the small advantages she is too well-bred to ask for, and you take care, always, to do it from below, so that every favour reads as deference rather than threat. By now she leaves the door of her side of the room open when you are in it, which is the closest a Mei comes to letting you in."},{"k":"n","t":"So when the thing with Rhys comes to a head, she brings it to you."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"She drops onto the end of your bunk uninvited, turning her phone over in her hands. “Rhys asked me. Properly. Dinner, his parents’ box at the symphony, the whole production.” She says it the way you would describe a transaction you are not sure you want. “He’s sweet. He’s well-connected. He rows with Hosea, he’s in every room Hosea’s in.” A pause that gives her away completely. “And he is not Hosea.”"},{"k":"n","t":"There it is, in the open: the medical heiress wants the Student President, the Student President has shown no sign of wanting her, and Rhys—Hosea’s closest friend, his shadow in every room—is the consolation prize that happens to come with access."},{"k":"n","t":"You can see the whole board. If Thea takes Rhys, then Thea is Rhys’s, and Rhys is Hosea’s, and you are Thea’s roommate-and-confidante, and the four of you become a unit by simple arithmetic—which puts you, at last, in every room Hosea is in, without ever having to engineer your way there. The bridge to Hosea is Rhys. And the toll on the bridge is Thea’s happiness, which is not your concern, and which you are about to spend."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“You’re the only one I can ask without it getting back to anyone. Should I say yes to him?”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Thea has brought you the bridge to Hosea and asked whether she should cross it first. What do you tell her?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Tell her to wait for Hosea—do not settle for the friend when the real thing might still happen."},{"key":"B","t":"Tell her to take Rhys—frame it as the smart play: being on Hosea’s closest friend’s arm keeps her one handshake from Hosea, far better than waiting alone."},{"key":"C","t":"Tell her it is hers to decide—stay out of it, give no steer either way."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Don’t settle for the shadow when you might still get the man. Wait for Hosea.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“…You’re right. Why would I take second place.” She turns Rhys down the next day."},{"k":"n","t":"And with that, the bridge collapses before you ever set foot on it: no Rhys means no foursome, no foursome means no natural way into Hosea’s circle, and you are back to being a roommate Thea tolerates, with the door to Hosea no closer than the day you arrived."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Thea","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Take him. Not because he’s the one—because the smartest thing you can do is stop waiting in a corner for Hosea to notice you, and start standing in every room he’s in. Rhys is those rooms. You don’t win Hosea by pining. You win him by being unavoidable. Rhys makes you unavoidable.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"Something clears in her face—you have handed her ambition a respectable shape, made the consolation prize sound like strategy. “That’s exactly it. It’s not settling. It’s positioning.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“It’s your call. I’m not going to push you either way.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“God, you’re useless when I actually need you.” She decides on her own, slowly, and whichever way she lands she does not credit you with it—and you have spent three weeks of deference and gotten nothing back when it counted, because you declined to be useful in the one moment she would have remembered."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"Thea says yes to Rhys that weekend, and the board reorganises exactly as you read it."},{"k":"n","t":"Within days there is a we—Thea and Rhys, and Rhys drags Hosea everywhere Rhys goes, and Thea drags you everywhere Thea goes, and so the four of you are suddenly a standing arrangement: dinners, the rowing socials, the long loose afternoons where the people who matter at Concord decide who else gets to matter. You did not force a single door. You made yourself indispensable to the girl holding the key, and steered her toward the boy who was the bridge, and walked across it on her arm."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"She watches you leave for the first of these dinners, dressed in the best you own, and there is something careful in her face."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“You got Thea to take Rhys. Which puts you at every table Hosea sits at. Which is, I’m fairly sure, the whole reason you told her to take him.” It is not an accusation, quite. “I’m not judging. I’d just—I’d hate to watch you get so good at building bridges that you forget which side of the water you actually wanted to be on.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I know which side I want. The dry one. The one where Kye gets his surgery and I never have to count bus fare again.” You check your reflection, the borrowed-confidence version of yourself you wear to these things. “Everything between me and that side is just bridges, Veda. Some of them are people. I try not to think about it too hard.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“Try harder,” she says, gently, and lets you go."},{"k":"n","t":"At the dinner, Hosea is already there when you arrive on Thea’s arm, and his eyes find you the second you come through the door—the way they always do, the way that is starting to feel less like luck and more like a thing he allows you to notice—and you take your seat in his circle at last, exactly where you spent three weeks engineering yourself to sit, and you do not let your face show how much it cost to get here."}]},{"n":14,"title":"Friday","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The text comes Wednesday, from the number you still have not saved and still know on sight."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"Friday. Seven. The library annex. I’ll walk you through the joint-track material you will, without me, inevitably fail. Don’t be late. I despise lateness."},{"k":"n","t":"He has left campus. He is, you realise, arranging to come back on a Friday evening—from the company, from whatever a Vantage director does with his Fridays—to sit in a library annex and call you a moron for two hours. You tell yourself it is the free tutoring talking and you say yes, and you do not look too hard at the fact that a man that busy is rearranging a Friday for you."},{"k":"n","t":"Friday, six-fifteen, you are getting ready when Thea’s name lights your phone."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“Change of plan—Rhys booked the whole upstairs at Lumière, tonight, the four of us, and Hosea’s bringing the rowing eight. Everyone who matters this term is going to be in that room. You’re coming. Be downstairs in twenty.” A pause, bright and absolute. “It’s not optional. I didn’t spend a week getting you into this circle for you to skip the first real night of it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Seven o’clock. The library annex, where a silver-haired boy is going to be sitting with the joint-track material spread out, waiting. Or Lumière, the full table, Hosea, the room with everyone who matters in it—the room you spent three weeks building a bridge to reach."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"The room you built a bridge toward is open tonight. The appointment you promised Shiro is at seven. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Go to Lumière. Hosea is there, the whole circle is there; this is the night the bridge was built for. Stand Shiro up."},{"key":"B","t":"Keep the appointment with Shiro—he is coming back across the city for it, and something in you does not want to be the reason that seat sits empty."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You send Shiro nothing—no cancellation, no excuse, because an excuse is a conversation and you do not have time for one—and you go to Lumière, and the night is everything the bridge promised: Hosea’s eyes finding you across the table, the circle folding you in, your name learned by people who matter. You do not think about the annex. You make yourself not think about the annex."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I can’t tonight, I already—” but Thea is not a person you finish sentences at, and before you have found the end of yours, Veda is at your door with your coat, because Thea has texted Veda to bring you down, and Veda, who cannot say no to Thea any more than you can afford to, is already easing the coat onto your shoulders, apologising with her eyes."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“I’m sorry. She told me to get you. I didn’t know how to—”"},{"k":"n","t":"The coat is on. The decision is made by everyone except you. You meant to keep the appointment. You are, somehow, in the lift going down to Thea’s car anyway, and the annex recedes with every floor."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"Lumière is loud and gold and full of people whose names you are finally learning, and Hosea is across the table with his attention angled at you the way it always is now, and for a few hours you are exactly where three weeks of careful work was meant to put you."},{"k":"n","t":"Your phone stays in your bag. You do not take it out. You know there is nothing on it—he is not the kind to text where are you, not the kind to chase—and the silence from his end is somehow worse than a dozen messages would have been."},{"k":"n","t":"The library annex closes at eleven."},{"k":"n","t":"He is there at seven, the material laid out in his small precise hand, two chairs, the second one pulled out at an angle that suggests someone is expected. At seven-thirty he checks his phone once and puts it face-down. At eight he does not check it. He does not text you. He despises lateness and he says nothing about it to the one person being late, because saying something would be admitting the seat was for you and the evening was arranged around you and the city was crossed for you, and a Si does not admit to wanting a thing that did not come."},{"k":"n","t":"By nine the annex is empty but for him. By ten he has read the same page four times without taking any of it in. At eleven the librarian tells him they are closing, and he gathers the material he prepared for a student who never arrived, and squares it into a neat stack, and leaves."},{"k":"s","who":"Mason","t":"The next morning, carefully. “…So she didn’t show.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I had work to do regardless. The annex was as good a place as any.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Mason","t":"“You sat in a library for four hours doing ‘work’ you could’ve done at the office, waiting for a girl who stood you up without a text, and you’re going to pretend—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I’m going to pretend nothing, because nothing happened.” He does not look up. “She’s a busy student. Students have obligations. It is not my concern how she spends a Friday.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He says it flatly, and Mason, who has known him long enough to read the thing under the flatness, says nothing more—because the thing under the flatness is a boy who crossed a city, set out two chairs, and is now explaining to himself, very calmly, why being left in an empty room for four hours does not bother him at all."}]},{"n":15,"title":"The Archipelago","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The archipelago lies four hundred kilometres off the Ecuadorian coast, volcanic islands flung up from the seabed before the continents had finished arranging themselves, and the society lands on it with sterilised boots and the standing instruction that nothing organic comes ashore and nothing organic leaves. Hosea runs the trip; the patron, predictably, is a continent away, and his absence is a thing you notice and decline to examine."},{"k":"n","t":"What isolation has made here is endemism of an order found almost nowhere: species descended from a handful of ancestors that arrived by accident and diversified across the islands, evolving in a world without predators—and so evolving no fear of them."},{"k":"n","t":"You log it the way you log everything. A marine reptile basking on cooled lava, returned from a foraging dive in water cold enough to slow a body that runs on the sun. A ground bird that walks up to the survey party without alarm, because it descends from ancestors who never met a thing worth fleeing, and that absence of fear reads, to you, not as tameness but as the visible cost of a habitat that never demanded vigilance. A seabird colony at a density the mainland could never feed—abundant, breeding, doomed the instant a rat or a cat or a careless boot arrives, because the same fearlessness that lets them thrive is what will erase them. Isolation is the asset and the death sentence in one trait."},{"k":"n","t":"Hosea walks the survey beside you, and listens to you reason, and the listening is its own kind of courtship."},{"k":"n","t":"You find the creature on the third afternoon, in the scrub above the lava: a small animal, wrong-angled, a wing broken, too young and too still, the kind of thing the island’s indifference would finish by nightfall."},{"k":"n","t":"And you know—the cold strategic part of you knows instantly—that the path back to the boats runs right past this spot, and that Hosea will come down it within the hour."},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"A wounded animal is also a stage, if you choose to make it one. How do you handle it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Tend it loudly and visibly—position yourself where he cannot miss the sight of the hard, clever girl revealing her soft hidden heart."},{"key":"B","t":"Just tend it. Crouch in the scrub, do the actual work of warming and steadying a dying animal, and let him come upon it however he comes upon it."},{"key":"C","t":"Carry it down to him directly—present the wounded thing, let him see you need help, let the rescue be a shared moment from the start."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":-5},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You arrange yourself in the open where the path bends, the wounded creature cradled just so, your face composed into tender concern, and you wait to be discovered being good."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He comes down the path, and he sees it—the tableau, staged a half-degree too perfectly—and something in his expression cools even as it smiles. “You found a hurt animal at the exact spot I’d walk past, and you’re holding it like a painting.” Light, almost fond, and entirely unfooled. “You don’t have to perform softness for me. I’d have believed the real thing.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You overplayed your one good card, and he read it face-up."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":15},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You crouch in the scrub and do the work—warm the small body, splint the wing with a clipboard clip and a strip torn from your sleeve—and you do it well, with the steady competent tenderness that looks, from the path, exactly like a hard girl’s hidden soft heart."},{"k":"n","t":"It is not. You know precisely what you are doing: a wounded animal is the most efficient instrument of good ever invented, and you are using it. There is a flicker of real reflex under it—you do not like watching things die—but reflex is not attachment, and this is a tool with a pulse, not a thing you will let yourself need."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He comes down the path and stops, and watches you not notice him, and what he sees—the version you did not stage—is the one that undoes him. He reads it as the truth beneath your armour."},{"k":"n","t":"He has read it exactly wrong."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“…You didn’t see me there,” he says quietly. “That’s the first unguarded thing I’ve watched you do.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":3},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You carry it down and hold it out to him, wounded and trembling, and ask for his help."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He helps—of course he helps—but his eyes have already catalogued the choreography: the girl who finds a hurt thing and brings it straight to the one man she has been circling, turning a rescue into an introduction. “We’ll splint it on the boat,” he says, kind and a little remote, and the remoteness is the sound of a man who has seen the move before."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The creature lives. You smuggle it past biosecurity the way you smuggle everything past everyone—carefully, against the rules—and by the time the boats reach the mainland it has decided you are the world."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“What will you call it?”"},{"k":"n","t":"You look at the small thing breathing against your palm. You know exactly what it is: a tool with a pulse, half-instinct and half-strategy, useful for precisely as long as it stays useful. Not a thing you need. Not a thing you would grieve. If the day came that it cost you something, you would set it down without breaking stride."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Solace.” You say it lightly, with an irony only you can hear—naming a tool comfort, in a life built entirely of bridges and debts."},{"k":"n","t":"Hosea takes the name as proof of the soft thing he believes he has found in you. He thinks he has seen your underbelly. He has seen your best performance, and he is already, quietly, building plans on the mistake—a man courting a girl’s tenderness that does not, where he thinks it lives, exist."},{"k":"n","t":"That night your phone finds signal, and a message loads, sent days ago, never delivered until now:"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"The annex was closing so I left. The material’s in your desk drawer at the society. You’ll need it. Don’t thank me."},{"k":"n","t":"No reproach. No where were you. Just the work, left where you would find it. You read it once, put the phone away, and feel the small cold thing you feel whenever this particular person does something you cannot fit into the box you have put him in—the box marked useful, cold, irrelevant. He keeps not staying in it."}]},{"n":16,"title":"The Invitation","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The society meeting after the archipelago trip should have been routine: minutes, specimen logs, a schedule for the next survey, the kind of orderly campus administration that lets important people feel virtuous about leaving the room on time. Cassia waits until the last item is done before she ruins it."},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"“Before everyone goes—Shiro Si’s birthday is this Saturday. His family is hosting on the marina yacht, and the society received a block of invitations.” She smiles in the public, generous way that means she has already chosen where the blade enters. “It would be strange not to include everyone who travelled with us.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The invitation lands in your hand like a trap dressed as paper. Public enough that refusing looks like fear. Personal enough that accepting puts you on a deck where Cassia can make you visible to the one person most likely to ask who let you in."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Cassia has offered the invitation where everyone can hear. How do you answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Refuse outright. You have no reason to attend a Si birthday party."},{"key":"B","t":"Accept too eagerly, as if the yacht itself is the prize."},{"key":"C","t":"Accept cleanly, and make Cassia share the risk of having invited you."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I don’t think that’s necessary.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"“Oh.” Her sympathy is immediate, polished, fatal. “Of course. Not everyone is comfortable in that setting.”"},{"k":"n","t":"By dinner the version travelling fastest is not that you declined a trap. It is that you knew better than to show your face where you would not belong."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“That sounds wonderful. I’d love to go.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"Her smile brightens by a fraction. Too easy. You have made yourself look hungry for the exact room she wants to see reject you, and now she can enjoy every second of leading you to it."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Thank you, Cassia. It’s generous of you to include me. I’ll make sure everyone remembers you were the one who did.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Cassia","t":"The smile stays. The pleasure does not. “Of course.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The meeting breaks up. Cassia has lost the clean shot—if you go down on that deck, she goes down half a step with you, the named sponsor of a public disaster at the patron’s party. She still wants you there. She just cannot be seen enjoying it anymore."},{"k":"n","t":"Which leaves the smaller, more humiliating problem: you have nothing to wear to a ninety-foot yacht. You have exactly one person to ask."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"She takes your call with a queen’s boredom and a queen’s curiosity both. “You need a dress. For a yacht. The Si boy’s birthday—I saw the marina booking, half the daughters in this city are sick they weren’t asked.” A pause. “Why are you going.”"},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"Vivienne can give you a dress. She can also give you much more than that, if she misunderstands correctly. How do you answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Tell her the truth—a society obligation, a member cornered you into it, you have to show your face."},{"key":"B","t":"Say nothing, and let her draw her own conclusion."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Society thing. A member made it impossible to skip. Showing up is less bad than not.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"“Mm. How dreary.” Half her interest evaporates, and she has a perfectly adequate dress couriered over with the warmth of a tax rebate—you get the gown and none of the formidable goodwill that comes with Vivienne being invested."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Vivienne","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"You say nothing. You let the silence do what silence does."},{"k":"s","who":"Vivienne","t":"And Vivienne, who has watched girls chase boys since she was fourteen, fills it herself. “…Oh. Oh. You’re going for him. The cold one, the one who looked through you at the forum. You absolute lunatic.” Her voice has warmed completely—delighted, scandalised. “A woman possessed. Fine—I am not sending you in something adequate. If you’re going to throw yourself at a boy who can’t stand you, you’ll do it looking like the worst mistake he ever made.” You can already hear the wardrobe sliding open. “Come at noon. We’re going to ruin him.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"You do not correct her. Let Vivienne believe you have lost your head over Shiro Si—it costs you nothing and earns you everything: Vivienne wants Hosea, and she will never lift a finger against a girl circling the other boy, and a Vivienne with her wardrobe open and her mind made up is worth a hundred couriered dresses."},{"k":"n","t":"You hang up with a yacht invitation, a noon fitting, and a society rival bound uncomfortably to your fate. Vivienne’s wardrobe is sliding open."},{"k":"n","t":"The yacht is ahead."}]},{"n":17,"title":"The Yacht","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Saturday, four o’clock, and Thea’s family car is idling at the dorm gate with three seats filled and a fourth that is, suddenly, not for you."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"She does not even lower the window all the way. “Oh—we’re completely full, the boys took the back. You’ll make your own way, won’t you? It’s not far.” The smile is gracious and the meaning is not: you are not riding to the patron’s yacht in my family’s car."},{"k":"n","t":"Rhys looks vaguely uncomfortable in the back. Hosea is not in this car. Wren, in the third seat, does not meet your eyes."},{"k":"n","t":"You are standing on the curb in Vivienne’s dress—and the dress is a weapon, you will give her that—being told to find your own way to a marina forty minutes out."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Thea has stranded you at the curb in front of everyone. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Call it what it is—“You said we’d go together. Don’t pretend the car’s full when I can count the seats.”"},{"key":"B","t":"Smile, wave them off, and tell them you’ll see them there—take the humiliation without letting it show, and find your own way."},{"key":"C","t":"Look wounded, hover at the window, make her feel the cruelty until she is shamed into squeezing you in."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Thea","delta":-10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“You said we’d go together. I can count, Thea—there’s a seat.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“God, don’t make it awkward.” The window goes up. The car pulls away, and now you are the girl who made a scene at the curb over a lift, and Thea has a fresh grievance to share with everyone at the party before you even arrive."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Of course—go ahead, I’ll see you there.” You wave them off like it costs you nothing, and the second the car turns the corner you flag a taxi you cannot afford and watch the meter eat a week’s wages on the coast road, jaw set, dress immaculate, arriving on your own terms or not at all."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Thea","delta":-5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"You linger at the window, let your face fall, let the hurt show."},{"k":"s","who":"Thea","t":"“…Ugh, fine,” she says, and makes Wren shove over, and you ride to the marina wedged in a car where everyone now knows you guilted your way in—arriving as the girl who could not take a hint, which is a worse entrance than no entrance at all."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The taxi costs what it costs. You arrive late—the party has started without you, and you can hear it from the gangway, the particular roar of a room full of people who all matter, glasses and music and four hundred conversations pitched to be overheard."},{"k":"n","t":"You hand your invitation to the crew at the rail. You step through the doors into the saloon."},{"k":"n","t":"And the room turns."},{"k":"n","t":"It is the dress, partly—Vivienne did not send you in adequate—but it is also the simple arithmetic of an unknown girl arriving alone and late and unbothered, and for one long moment the attention of the entire party settles on you, sticky and assessing, the daughters of the city recalculating who you are and why they do not know you."},{"k":"n","t":"For exactly that moment, you are the most looked-at person on the boat."},{"k":"n","t":"Then a voice comes down from above, flat and cold, and the moment shatters."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He is standing at the top of the stairs to the upper deck, looking down at you across the whole bright room, and there is nothing in his face but a distant, arctic distaste. “Who let you in.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The party goes quiet in pieces. Heads turn from you to him and back."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“It’s my birthday. So I’ll ask you to leave.” He says it without heat, which is worse than heat. “I would simply rather not look at refuse on the one day of the year I’m obliged to host. It puts me off the cake.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Somewhere near the bar, Cassia is watching with a satisfaction she is failing to hide—this is the picture she sent you here for, the supplemental nobody put in her place in front of everyone—and she does not know, cannot know, that the boy delivering the blow rearranged his life to sit beside you in a lecture hall three weeks running."},{"k":"n","t":"You do not know it either. To you this is simply a person who has, for reasons you cannot fathom, decided to be cruel to you in public, and who keeps not making sense—the cold director, the four-hour wait in the annex, the seat beside you, and now refuse, on the one day of the year. None of his pieces fit together, and you have stopped trying to make them."},{"k":"n","t":"You have one thing in your bag you brought to give him. You have a room full of witnesses. And you have about three seconds to decide how to leave."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"Shiro has asked you to leave in front of the whole room. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Defend yourself—name Cassia, name the invitation, make clear you did not crash his party."},{"key":"B","t":"Apologise and retreat fast, get off the boat before it gets worse."},{"key":"C","t":"Say nothing in your defence—leave the thing you brought, and go with your spine straight."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Cassia invited me. The whole society was—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I didn’t ask who handed you a card. I asked who let you think it meant you were welcome.” Cold, final. The room watches you explain yourself to a man who has already turned away, and explaining is what prey does, and the picture Cassia wanted is complete."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’m sorry—I’ll go—” and you turn too fast, and the retreat is a small visible collapse, and the room files you as a girl who folded the instant a Si raised his voice. Cassia could not have scripted it better."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You do not explain. You do not apologise. You hold his cold eyes for one beat, long enough that the room sees you decline to crumble, and then you take the thing you brought out of your bag, set it down on the table by the door without a word about what it is, turn, and walk out with your borrowed dress and your unborrowed spine intact."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"You leave it on the table—a small wrapped thing, no card, no explanation—and you walk back out onto the gangway into the salt wind, and you do not let the party see you shake."},{"k":"n","t":"Behind you, the room exhales, the music resumes, and Shiro Si looks at the small wrapped object you left on the table by the door, and tells the nearest crew member, flatly, to throw it out."},{"k":"n","t":"What happens after that, you do not see. You are already on the dark gangway, deciding whether the taxi fare home is worth it or whether you will walk, when you hear—faintly, impossibly—someone come crashing out of the saloon behind you, and your name, in a voice you have never once heard that man use."}]},{"n":18,"title":"The Score","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You are halfway down the gangway, deciding whether the taxi home is worth what is left in your account, when the saloon doors bang open behind you and your name comes across the dark water in a voice you have never once heard that man use."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Sealyra.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You turn, and he is coming down the gangway too fast, and the arctic composure you have watched him wear in every room is simply gone—peeled off, nowhere on his face—and in his hand, crushed, is the thing you left on the table: the score, the sheet music, unwrapped now, the black-and-white staves catching the dock lights."},{"k":"n","t":"His hand closes around your wrist hard enough to bruise."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Where.” His chest is heaving as though he ran much further than the length of a deck, and there is a wet brightness at the rims of his eyes that you have no framework for. “Where did you get this.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"You are too startled by the state of him to manage anything but the truth. “A—a friend wrote it. Years ago.” You have never seen him lose a single degree of control, and watching it come off him all at once frightens you more than any of his cruelty did. “Why—what is wrong with you?”"},{"k":"n","t":"Something crosses his face at what is wrong with you—your bafflement, your obvious not-knowing—and it lands on him like cold water, and you watch him try to claw the composure back over the wound and only half manage it."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“A friend.” He says the word like it cut him on the way out. “You took a thing a person wrote with everything he had, carried it around for years, and then handed it to a stranger as a party favour—because you had nothing else in your bag.” His grip does not loosen. “Come with me.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He does not wait for an answer. He pulls you off the gangway, back through a side door, into a small cabin away from the party, and shuts it, and the noise of four hundred people falls to nothing."},{"k":"n","t":"There is a piano in the cabin—the boat kept one, the way the society kept one, from whatever it used to be. He sets the crushed score on the stand and steps back."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Play it.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“What?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“You said a friend wrote it. So you know it. Play it for me.” His voice has gone quiet and terrible. “Every note.”"},{"k":"n","t":"And because the room is small and his eyes are red and you do not understand any of this, you sit, and you set your hands on the keys, and you begin to play the piece your fingers have known cold for three years."},{"k":"n","t":"You get four bars before his hand comes down flat on the keys, killing the sound, and he is gripping the edge of the piano like the floor has tilted."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Stop. Stop.” The breath he drags in is uneven. “So you can play it from memory. You kept it for three years and you can play it from memory and you brought it here, to give to a man you think is nothing to you, because it was the only thing you owned worth wrapping.” A terrible quiet. “Do you have any idea—” He stops. Starts again, lower, the question that is the whole of him cracking open: “Are you insulting me? Or are you insulting him?”"},{"k":"n","t":"You do not know who him is. You do not know that him and the man in front of you are the same person, that the friend who wrote it years ago, across a screen, under a name you deleted, is standing in this cabin with his eyes gone red over a song he wrote for a girl who just handed it back to him as wrapping paper."},{"k":"n","t":"You only know that you are cornered, that he is unravelling in a way you cannot account for, and that you came here to stay in his good graces, not to detonate whatever this is. So you do the thing you do—you reach for the lever that works on men."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I don’t want you angry with me. I’ve been trying to make it right.” You take a small step toward him, voice gone soft, pliant. “I’m sorry—truly. Just don’t be angry. Whatever you want, I’ll do it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"It is a manoeuvre, and it is a good one, and he calls it."},{"k":"n","t":"His hand leaves the piano. He closes the distance you opened. His fingers find your jaw and tilt it up, and his head lowers, and his mouth stops an inch from yours—testing, exactly, how far whatever you want goes."},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"He has called your lever and is testing what your apology actually means. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Shove him off. The offer was a lever, not a literal thing—you did not mean this."},{"key":"B","t":"Hold still. Do not flinch. Let him find out precisely how far it goes."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You shove him back before his mouth reaches yours."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He stops. He straightens. The wildness drains out of his face and leaves a flat, terrible calm. “So it wasn’t true. Whatever you want.” Quiet, almost to himself. “It was never true. None of it is.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He picks the crushed score off the stand, crosses to the open porthole, and tears it once, twice, and lets the night wind take the pieces out over the black water."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Wait—” You are across the cabin before you have decided to move. “Wait—”"},{"k":"n","t":"The pieces are gone. So, when you turn, is he—the cabin door standing open on an empty corridor, the song he wrote you dissolving into the sea."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Shiro","delta":5},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You do not move. You do not flinch. You let him come the whole way—and an inch from your mouth, it is his nerve, not yours, that fails."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He stops. “So it is true.” Wondering, almost. “You’d let me do anything. To stay in my good graces.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"Your heart is slamming—rage or fear, you cannot tell. “Obviously not that.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"Something shutters back over his face, colder for having cracked. “Ah. A paid service, then. With terms.” He takes out his billfold and counts ten red notes onto the piano lid without looking at them. “How much. Will this cover it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You pick the notes up, one by one. You square them into a neat stack. You slide them back into his billfold, close it, and hold it out to him."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Don’t throw money around like that. Your parents worked hard for it.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"The billfold does not move toward your hand. Something detonates behind his eyes—not anger, or not only; a wound pressed all the way down—because the money is not his parents’, it is his, earned by a body that was never supposed to live this long, in a family that never once looked at him the way you just assumed they did. You have, with one careless kindness, put your thumb on every bruise he owns and called it a small one."},{"k":"n","t":"He does not take the billfold. He crosses to the porthole instead, with the score, and tears it, and gives it to the sea."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“So—am I allowed to stay?”"},{"k":"n","t":"The answer is the cabin door slamming hard enough to rattle the frame."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"You stand alone in the cabin, the billfold or the empty air still in your hand, the porthole open on the dark where a stranger’s song is dissolving into salt."},{"k":"n","t":"You do not understand what just happened. You understand only that you wounded him—precisely, fatally, without aiming—and that no one bleeds like that over a sheet of music a girl he claims not to know handed him at a party."},{"k":"n","t":"He hates you enough to tear it up and throw it to the sea. He looked, for a moment, like the tearing cost him a limb."},{"k":"n","t":"Both are true. You cannot make them sit together, and you have stopped being able to ignore the not-fitting. You step out of the cabin to find your way off this boat."},{"k":"n","t":"Outside, the deck has begun to pitch, and the amber horizon is gone, overrun, and the storm that no one promised is already here."}]},{"n":19,"title":"The Toast","lead":"hosea","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You come back to the party the way you come back to everything—face rebuilt, spine relocked, the cabin and the red-eyed boy and the song dissolving in the sea all folded down small and shoved somewhere you will deal with later. But your hands are still cold from where his shook around your wrist, and the music in the saloon is too loud, and the lights are too gold, and you cannot quite get your heartbeat to believe the danger has passed."},{"k":"n","t":"Thea’s table finds you before you can find a wall to stand against. Rhys, flushed and loud; Thea, bright and watching; two of the rowing boys; and Hosea at the end of it, who looks up when you arrive and does not look away, and whose attention you feel land on you like a hand on the back of your neck."},{"k":"n","t":"Then one of the rowing boys—the kind of boy who has never once been told no and has built a whole personality on the absence—pushes a full glass across the table at you."},{"k":"s","who":"Rowing Boy","t":"“The new girl hasn’t toasted the patron. That’s rude, isn’t it. On his birthday.” A grin with teeth in it. “Drink. To Si. All the way down.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The table turns to you. It is not really about the toast. It is the oldest game these people play—make the one without money do a small humiliating thing, and watch whether she knows she has no standing to refuse. The glass is full to the brim. The smell of it reaches you, sharp and expensive, and you can feel Thea watching to see what you are made of, and you can feel, at the edge of it, Hosea going very still."},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"The table has made the toast a test. How do you get through it?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Drink it. Down it, all of it, prove you can hang—do not give them the satisfaction of watching you flinch."},{"key":"B","t":"Decline lightly—“I don’t drink, sorry”—and let yourself look a little soft, a little out of place, rather than make it a fight."},{"key":"C","t":"Refuse hard—“No”—flat, no apology, no inch given."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You lift the glass and drink, and it burns, and you are three swallows down when a hand closes over the rim and takes it gently out of yours."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"“That’s enough. She doesn’t have to empty a glass to prove anything to you.” Easy, smiling, but his hand is still around the glass that was at your mouth, and the whole table has just watched the Student President reach across three people to take a drink away from the supplemental girl as though her getting drunk were his concern."},{"k":"n","t":"The protectiveness is naked for one half-second too long. Rhys’s eyebrows go up. Thea’s smile sets. And somewhere across the saloon, a girl you have never been introduced to goes very, very still."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Hosea","delta":5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I don’t, actually—sorry. I’m a terrible drunk and I’d rather not be the one crying on a yacht.” You make it small, self-deprecating, a girl declining out of weakness rather than defiance, and you hand them the easy version of yourself to laugh at."},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He picks the moment with the lightness of a man who does this for a living. “Leave her—not everyone treats their liver like a rowing trophy. Someone get her something that won’t end the evening early.”"},{"k":"n","t":"It reads, to the table, as the Student President being decent to everyone, the way he is decent to everyone; nothing aimed, nothing owed. The boys laugh. The moment dissolves. Hosea is just like that, the table decides, and moves on."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“No.” Flat. No softening, no apology."},{"k":"n","t":"The table’s good humour curdles. You have made a rowing boy lose face in front of his friends, and a boy like that does not forgive a no with no padding on it. The night turns subtly colder around you, and you have spent goodwill you could not afford to make a point you did not need to make."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The moment passes. The table laughs, the toast forgotten, and Hosea catches your eye for half a second—you alright?—and you give him nothing back but the small flat look that means don’t, because the worst thing he can do for you in this room is be kind to you where people can see."},{"k":"n","t":"But it is already too late, and you do not know it yet."},{"k":"n","t":"Across the saloon, half-shadowed by the bar, stands a girl. You have never spoken to her. You would not know her name—Faye, though it will not matter soon—but she knows yours, because she has been watching Hosea Su the way the devout watch a relic since the first week of term, and she was on the autumn intensive, and she walked the same wet paths you walked, and she saw, on a lava shore four hundred kilometres from here, the way the Student President’s eyes followed a supplemental nobody who was supposed to be beneath all of them."},{"k":"n","t":"She has spent weeks telling herself she imagined it. And she has just watched him reach across a table to take a glass out of your hand like your safety was the only thing in the room, and the imagining has curdled into something with a pulse and a temperature, and it is looking at you now across the gold light with an expression that has nothing left to do with admiration."},{"k":"n","t":"You feel none of this. You are looking at your own cold hands, thinking about a boy in a cabin and a song in the sea."},{"k":"n","t":"The storm takes the boat an hour later."},{"k":"n","t":"It comes the way these things come—amber to overrun in minutes, the deck tilting, rain sideways, the crew herding everyone toward the saloon in a press of bodies and shouting. You are at the rail, working your way in with the rest, the wind tearing at Vivienne’s ruined dress, when through the chaos and the wet a shape comes up close behind you, and you catch—too late, in the half-second before it happens—the smell of someone’s perfume and the heat of someone who has come much too near."},{"k":"n","t":"A voice, low, only for you, under the storm:"},{"k":"s","who":"Faye","t":"“He never looks at anyone. Do you know that? Never. Until you.”"},{"k":"n","t":"And then the flat hard press of two hands between your shoulder blades, and the rail is gone from under your grip, and the deck surrenders the soles of your feet."},{"k":"n","t":"The world tilts, and the black water rushes up to take you, and your heart, in the instant before the cold swallows everything, simply stops."},{"k":"n","t":"A hand breaks the surface above you, reaching down into the dark, and closes around your wrist."}]},{"n":20,"title":"The Hand","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"The hand closes around your wrist in the black, and it is the only warm thing in a world that has gone entirely to cold and dark and the roar of water in your ears, and you do not yet know whose it is."},{"k":"n","t":"He should not be able to do this."},{"k":"n","t":"The instant Shiro’s head goes under, it is not this water. It is a hand fisted in a child’s hair, forcing him down; it is wrists bound, ankles bound, six years old and unable to thrash against the dark pouring into his lungs; it is the oldest terror he owns, the one he has organised his entire adult life around never feeling again—and it is here, now, closing over him in the cold, and for one white instant the most controlled person in the city is a drowning boy who never came all the way up from the last time."},{"k":"n","t":"And he goes down into it anyway. For you."},{"k":"n","t":"He finds your wrist. He drags himself up through the memory by main force, and breaks the surface with you, and the first thing you understand about the hand is that it is shaking—shaking violently, the tremor making a lie of the strength somehow pouring out of it."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Hold on to me.” Rain and seawater in his mouth, the words coming ragged. “Do you hear me—I said hold on to me!”"},{"k":"n","t":"You try. Your hands will not close right; the cold has unstrung them, and fear has done the rest, and you feel yourself slipping even as he hauls. And this close, in the lightning-flickers, you see his face: lips gone a white the cold cannot account for, the light in his eyes guttering like a flame in wind, on the edge of going out altogether."},{"k":"n","t":"He knows what it is. Of everyone on that boat, he is the one person who knows exactly what the white crawling into the edges of his sight means, what the heart slamming itself to pieces against his ribs is doing—a fever he ignored for days because some buried part of him stopped minding whether he lived, and now this, the one exertion every doctor he ever paid told him would kill him."},{"k":"n","t":"Why now, is the last whole thought he is able to hold, hauling a girl who handed him his own torn-up heart an hour ago. Of every moment in my life—why does it have to be now."},{"k":"n","t":"He gets you to the platform. He spends everything a failing heart was never carrying, and the crew’s hands close on you and the boards come up under you and you are aboard—gasping, retching the sea back out, alive."},{"k":"n","t":"You turn to find him."},{"k":"n","t":"He is behind you—the crew have him under the arms, hauling him up over the rail, and for one flooding second of relief he is out, he is on his feet on the platform, soaked and grey and alive, coughing the water out of his lungs. He made it. You both made it."},{"k":"n","t":"And then, standing there—out of the sea, safe, the danger ostensibly over—he goes down."},{"k":"n","t":"Not into the water. Onto the boards."},{"k":"n","t":"One hand flies to his chest and the other finds nothing to hold and he folds, knees first, the way a building comes down when the thing holding it up has finally been asked for more than it had. His face has gone past pale into something waxen, lips tinged the wrong colour, and his breath is coming in short snatched gulps that do not seem to reach anywhere."},{"k":"n","t":"You are on your knees beside him before you have decided to be."},{"k":"s","who":"Crew Member","t":"“It’s his heart—get the kit—he has a condition, the family flagged it, it’s not the drowning, it’s his heart—”"},{"k":"n","t":"The words rearrange the world. Not the drowning. The sea did not do this. He did—he took a body that was already running a fever he had ignored for days, a heart scarred years ago by something no one on this boat will say out loud, and he threw it into cold water twice for you, and the heart has finally sent the bill."},{"k":"n","t":"He is half-conscious, grey, his hand fisted in the soaked silk of your ruined dress as though it is the last fixed point in a spinning world, and his eyes find yours for one unfocused second."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“…told you,” he gets out, barely sound at all, the ghost of the arrogance still running on fumes, “…to hold on.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Then they roll closed, and the crew is shouting, and they get him onto the board and you do not let go of his hand the whole way down to the medical bay, your cold fingers locked around his colder ones, not strategy, not calculation, nothing you will ever be able to justify to yourself afterward."},{"k":"n","t":"Below deck they work on him behind a curtain you are not allowed past. You sit on the floor of the corridor in a dress that cost more than your month and is ruined past saving, salt water pooling under you, and you hold the two facts you can no longer keep apart."},{"k":"n","t":"An hour ago he tore your song to pieces and gave it to the sea."},{"k":"n","t":"Twenty minutes ago he gave his heart to the same sea to keep you out of it."},{"k":"n","t":"You read people for a living. You have boxes for all of them—useful, dangerous, cold, irrelevant. You have no box that holds a boy who would drown your music over you and then nearly drown his own heart for you in the same hour, and the cold engine that has run you since you were thirteen turns it over and over and lands, at last, against every wall you built, on the conclusion you have been refusing since an empty seat in a lecture hall:"},{"k":"n","t":"No one spends a scarred heart twice, in a storm, for a girl who is nothing to him."},{"k":"n","t":"Across the deck above, white-faced and shaking, Faye watches them carry the patron down on a board. What is on her face is no longer jealousy. It is terror. She pushed a nobody into the sea—and watched the most powerful person on that boat nearly die hauling her out, and she is only now beginning to understand exactly how much you are worth to him, and exactly what she has set in motion."},{"k":"n","t":"The hand that pushed you is never found. The crew logs a guest overboard in heavy weather, recovered. No one saw anything but rain."},{"k":"n","t":"But you saw his hand fist in your dress like you were the last solid thing in the world. You are not going to be able to unsee that."}]},{"n":21,"title":"The Rumour","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"He is stable by Monday. You know because you call the private wing four times over the weekend and a nurse, eventually, takes pity and tells you so—stable, resting, no visitors authorised but family—and you hang up each time with your heart doing something undisciplined that you decline to name."},{"k":"n","t":"The campus, meanwhile, has built its monument."},{"k":"n","t":"By Monday the story has one irresistible shape: Shiro Si, the patron who attends nothing, who has never crossed a room for a living soul, went off a yacht in a storm with a failing heart to haul the supplemental-admission nobody out of the sea, and put himself in a hospital bed doing it. The gap between what he is and what he did for her is too wide for anyone to leave alone. In the dining hall, the society chats, the glances that trail you down every corridor—the same question, turned over and over: why her. Why would he. Of everyone—why her."},{"k":"n","t":"Cassia has gone quiet, which is its own kind of loud. Faye has not been seen. And Veda finds you at breakfast with the grim efficiency of a war correspondent."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“You have until lunch to decide what you are. Right now you’re a blank the whole school is filling in, and not one version they’re writing is kind.” She lowers her voice. “People keep asking me if you’ll deny it. Whether there’s ‘really anything there.’ What do I tell them.”"},{"k":"c","kind":"save","q":"The rumour is moving faster than you can. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Deny it flatly—make it known it was a reflex rescue, you and Si are nothing, and kill the story."},{"key":"B","t":"Say nothing. Let the rumour float; a thing this easy to disprove never needs disproving."},{"key":"C","t":"Lean into it—let them keep believing there is something between you and the most untouchable boy in the school."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You set the record straight to anyone who will listen: it meant nothing, he would have done it for a stranger, you and he are not a thing."},{"k":"n","t":"The trouble is who you are. A nobody announcing that a Si nearly died for her by accident, that it meant nothing, reads as either delusion or staggering vanity—and worse, it reaches him. Lying in a hospital bed with a heart that gave out hauling you in, he hears that you have told the school it was nothing. Whatever cracked open on that yacht, you have just frozen it shut from the outside, and a degree colder than the sea."}],"end":"END"},{"key":"B","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You tell Veda to say nothing, and you say nothing yourself—because it is simply the best move on the board. An unfed rumour bores itself to death; denying it would only feed it; and there is the practical floor under all of it, which is that publicly announcing a director nearly died fishing you out by accident, meant nothing, makes you look either delusional or heartless, and both cost you. Silence is just the cheapest square to stand on. Do not dress it up."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":null,"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You let it grow, and a few well-placed half-smiles later the whole campus is sure of it."},{"k":"n","t":"And so, fatally, is everyone you could not afford to convince—a nobody who lets a Si rumour bloom is a nobody announcing she climbs, and the climbers are the first ones a place like this throws back into the sea."}],"end":"END"}]},{"k":"n","t":"The text comes Tuesday afternoon, from the number you never saved and would know in your sleep."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"I’m told to rest and “be supervised.” I won’t be supervised by a paid stranger. You put me in this bed—be useful in it. Come to St. Aurelius, room 4, private wing. I’ll tutor you for the joint-track exams while I’m stuck here, since you’ll fail without me. The offer is more generous than you deserve and expires at six."},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“Who is it?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“The cold one. Billing me for the rescue. In tutoring.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Veda","t":"“Are you going?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Obviously.” You are already reaching for your bag. “I owe him—the arrogant little gremlin actually landed himself in a hospital fishing me out, so yes, there’s a debt, and I pay my debts because unpaid debts are leverage other people get to hold over me. Also the best tutoring in the city is free and on offer, and I have an exam that decides everything. I’d be an idiot to say no to either.” You shrug into your coat. “As for why he can’t just type come see me without wrapping it in a debt and an insult and a deadline—terminal case of being insufferable. Not my problem to cure.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You go because the debt needs clearing and the tutoring is worth having and you are a little curious, frankly, what a Si looks like when he is flat on his back and cannot do the cold-and-vertical routine. That is all. Do not make it more than it is."}]},{"n":22,"title":"The Apple","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"St. Aurelius, private wing, room 4. The door is ajar, and before you push it you hear two voices, one doing all the work."},{"k":"s","who":"Mason","t":"“—so you faked needing supervision to get her to come nurse you. You. The man who once made me reschedule his own father for ‘bandwidth.’” A low whistle. “The ‘you put me in this bed’ line, the tutoring bait—where does a man who’s never apologised in his life learn to—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I have myocarditis and I went into the sea. Nothing was faked.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Mason","t":"“You faked needing her, specifically. You turned down the actual nurse. Oh my god, the next line was supposed to make her offer to take care of you, wasn’t it—you studied this—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Get out, Mason.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He is laughing when he opens the door to leave and sees you standing there. His face does something delighted and doomed."},{"k":"s","who":"Mason","t":"“She’s here. Right. I’ll be—elsewhere.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He is gone."},{"k":"n","t":"Inside, Shiro is propped against white pillows, silver hair against white linen, paler than the bedding, and at the sight of you the arctic flatness slams down over his face as though the conversation you just heard never happened."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“We agreed on four. You are twenty-three minutes and twelve seconds late.” A beat. “I despise lateness.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You almost laugh. He nearly died four days ago and the first full sentence he is giving you is a stopwatch complaint."},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"He has opened with a punctuality complaint from a hospital bed. How do you answer?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Apologise properly—you are sorry, the bus only runs every half hour, it will not happen again."},{"key":"B","t":"Tease him—ask if he timed it on a stopwatch, since he has clearly got nothing better to do flat on his back."},{"key":"C","t":"Ignore the charge entirely and go straight for the fruit basket on the windowsill."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Shiro","delta":5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Sorry. The bus from campus runs every half hour. I’ll sort it next time.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“…Hm.” He looks faintly cheated of the argument he had loaded. “See that you do.”"}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Shiro","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Twenty-three minutes and twelve seconds. Did you lie there with a stopwatch, or does despising lateness just pass the time when you’ve got a hole in your heart and nothing to do?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"His mouth opens and produces nothing for a full second. “I have an accurate internal clock.”"},{"k":"n","t":"It is the worst recovery you have ever watched an intelligent person make, and you have to bite the inside of your cheek."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Shiro","delta":3},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You walk straight past the accusation to the fruit on the windowsill."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I am speaking to you about punctuality.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I heard you.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He is not used to being walked away from mid-grievance, and the novelty shuts him up."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]},{"k":"n","t":"You take an apple from the basket—small, faintly bruised, the cheap kind—and pick up the little knife."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Stop sulking. Let me peel you an apple. Consider it part of the debt.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He looks at the apple in your hand and does not open his mouth, and behind the flat face you can actually watch a war being fought."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"If I simply accept it, I let her off far too easily. And that apple is hideous—small, blemished, almost certainly cheap, very possibly the kind that gives a person food poisoning."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"But I arranged this entire convalescence specifically so that she would be here, attending to me. If I refuse to be attended to, the rescue was wasted and the illness was wasted. Still—accepting it without a fight is beneath my dignity. At minimum she ought to be on her knees with gratitude first."},{"k":"n","t":"You do not wait for the war to resolve. You peel it in four economical strokes, slice it, and hold a piece out on the flat of the knife."},{"k":"n","t":"He eats it. He eats it with the deep affront of a man whose principles have just lost a battle to a small ugly apple, chewing like every bite is a personal defeat, and says nothing, because there is nothing to say that survives the fact that he is, observably, eating the apple."},{"k":"n","t":"You do not point any of this out. It is, you will admit privately and to no one, the funniest thing you have seen all week."},{"k":"n","t":"Then he tutors you, and—this you did not expect—he is extraordinary at it. The cold goes out of him entirely the moment the subject is the work; every gap in your understanding located and filled before you have finished asking, the joint-track material laid out so cleanly you forget, for stretches, to be guarded. For one hour the boy who bills you for rescues is simply the best teacher you have ever sat across from. This, you note with pure mercenary satisfaction, is worth every minute of the gremlin’s attitude."},{"k":"n","t":"When the hour is done you gather your bag and stand."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Same time next week. I’ll be on time.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You start, unhurried, toward the door."},{"k":"c","kind":"affection","q":"The tutoring is over, and he is too proud to ask directly for company. What do you do?","opts":[{"key":"A","t":"Go—but slowly, and listen for what he does when he thinks you are leaving."},{"key":"B","t":"Tell him plainly you both know he did not bring you here for tutoring."},{"key":"C","t":"Just go. He nearly died; let him rest."}],"branches":[{"key":"A","aff":{"who":"Shiro","delta":10},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You slow your steps to half-speed, drawing the distance out, and you are nearly through the door when it comes from behind you."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"A low cough, deliberately stifled, the unmistakable sound of a man manufacturing a reason for you to turn around."},{"k":"n","t":"You turn. He is staring very fixedly at the window."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“…The seawater. It irritated the throat. You needn’t fuss.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Seawater. Right. I’ll bring throat lozenges next week, since you’re suffering so terribly.” You keep your face dead serious, and you do not say the thing you both know—that the cough arrived precisely when your hand touched the door, and that the most controlled person you have ever met just faked a symptom rather than say stay a bit longer."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“…Lozenges would be acceptable.” He is still staring at the window. The tips of his ears have gone faintly pink."},{"k":"n","t":"You leave with a peeled-apple’s worth of debt cleared, an hour of the best tutoring in the city banked, and the genuinely useful intelligence that the terrifying patron of the whole society will fake a cough at a closing door rather than admit he wants company. Filed under leverage, possibly. And under another heading you do not label, because labelling it would make it bigger than it is, and it is not big. He is an arrogant gremlin who saved your life and teaches like a god and cannot say stay. That is all. You catch your bus."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"B","aff":{"who":"Shiro","delta":-5},"blocks":[{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“You didn’t bring me here for tutoring, Mr. Si. We both know it.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"The flatness drops hard and fast. “I brought you to collect a debt. Do not mistake an invoice for an invitation.” He has retreated into the cold so completely you have lost whatever the apple gained."}],"end":"CONTINUE"},{"key":"C","aff":{"who":"Shiro","delta":3},"blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You go, because he is grey-faced and four days off a hospital admission and a person who nearly died is allowed to rest."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"Behind you a sound starts in his throat and is swallowed before it forms. By the time you have decided whether you heard it, the door has shut, and he is alone with a stifled cough and no one left to perform it for."}],"end":"CONTINUE"}]}]},{"n":23,"title":"The Lesson","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"You go back the next afternoon with a problem set under your arm and a different kind of agenda folded inside it."},{"k":"n","t":"Because the cabin won’t leave you alone. The torn score, the red rims of his eyes, are you insulting me, or are you insulting him—a man does not come apart like that over a sheet of music a stranger handed him, and the part of you that has survived on reading people has been turning it over for three days and getting nowhere. You can’t ask him directly. But he’s bored, and bedridden, and he owes you a tutoring session, and people say things when they think they’re only complaining."},{"k":"n","t":"So you sit down beside the bed and you push the hardest problem across the blanket."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Mr. Si. This one’s beyond me. I can’t do it.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He finishes the spoonful of broth he’s been nursing before he so much as glances at the page. “And?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“You said you’d tutor me.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“I didn’t set the paper.” He says it to the ceiling. “Your incompetence isn’t a syllabus item.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“It needs middle-school groundwork I don’t have. I spent those years working two jobs, not studying.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"The corner of his mouth curls, cold. “Working. Is that what we’re calling it. You spent middle school the way you spend everything—angling for the next thing, I’d imagine, not learning long division.”"},{"k":"n","t":"It’s a dig, and it’s also a door. He’s curious about you in spite of himself; you can use that. The question is which way to push."},{"k":"n","t":"**Choice**"},{"k":"n","t":"A. Defend yourself—explain that something happened at home, you had no choice."},{"k":"n","t":"B. Don’t defend. Turn it around and provoke him—needle the sheltered rich boy who couldn’t possibly understand."},{"k":"n","t":"C. Play pitiful—make him feel cruel for needling a girl who had it hard."},{"k":"n","t":"A"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Something happened at home. I didn’t get a choice about working.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"His eyes finally come off the ceiling. “Something happened. What.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You think of Kye, of the hospital ledger, of the one wound you keep behind every other thing you’ll let a person see, and you shake your head. He watches you close it, and his mouth thins."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Can’t invent one fast enough?”"},{"k":"n","t":"You’ve defended yourself onto your own back foot, and learned nothing about him, and handed him a soft spot to press besides."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"B"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"You let a small, knowing smile cross your face instead. “You’re right. A boy like you—raised somewhere with no weather in it—you couldn’t possibly understand.” You tilt your head, gentle, almost pitying. “Let me guess what your tragedies looked like at that age. A teacher kept you late. You lost a game. You came home after dark and got a lecture about it.” A beat. “Honestly? I envy it. Never having to grow up in a hurry. It sounds like a lovely way to be a child.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"The hand holding the problem set tightens, and a crease climbs out from one corner of the page with a small, audible crack."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"C"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“It was hard, okay? Harder than you’d know.” You let your voice waver."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Spare me.” Flat, bored, unmoved. “Helplessness is a strategy and a transparent one. I’ve watched better actresses run it.” He goes back to his broth, and the door you were prying at swings shut."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"For a moment you think you’ve overplayed it. Then his face does the opposite of what you expected—the anger drains out of it, slow, replaced by something quieter and far more dangerous, and he looks at you for a long beat as though deciding whether you’re worth an honest sentence."},{"k":"n","t":"He lies back against the pillows. Lifts one hand toward the ceiling, idle, studying the plain silver band on his smallest finger."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“It wasn’t quite a lovely way to be a child.” The lightness in his voice is a thin lacquer over something else. “Not exactly smooth sailing.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"Your pulse ticks. “Oh? The great Si heir’s life saw weather after all?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Not just weather. The boat went over.” Lazy, almost amused, eyes still on the ring. “Capsized badly. Came very close to sinking, the whole thing.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He says it the way you’d describe someone else’s misfortune, and that—the distance in it—is somehow worse than if he’d flinched."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“This—” a small gesture at his own chest, at the heart that gave out four days ago, “—I got it then. There was a stretch where it wasn’t clear I’d keep it. The heart, or the rest of me.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“…It was that serious?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“It relapses. Easily. Every time it does I can’t eat, can’t sleep, can’t do much but wait for it to decide whether I’m allowed to stay.” His eyes slide sideways to you, unhurried, and the next sentence is laid down with great care. “It had been stable for a long while. Until I went into the sea to fish out something that had been thrown there.”"},{"k":"n","t":"And there it is—the timeline, his hand around the ring, the torn score, the him—and the cold pattern-matching part of you that never switches off is laying the pieces side by side and not liking the shape they’re starting to make. The boat that went over. The thing he got back then. The song he came apart over. You don’t have the last piece. You’re not sure you want it. But you’ve stopped being able to pretend the pieces aren’t reaching for each other."},{"k":"n","t":"Then he doubles over."},{"k":"n","t":"The cough comes from somewhere deep and wrong, wracking, the kind that sounds like it’s trying to bring up a lung, and his face goes from pale to ash—cough, cough, cough—and your strategy and your suspicion and your half-formed pattern all evaporate at once, because whatever he is to you, he’s also a person whose heart stopped on a deck four days ago, and you’re already on your feet."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Are you alright—I’ll get the doctor—” You’re around the bed, hand flat on his back, the way you’d steady Kye—"},{"k":"n","t":"A hand comes up between you and the door."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Don’t.” The cough subsides. His voice is rough but level. “It’s nothing. Old memory. The feelings get away from me sometimes.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"Are you a tubercular heroine in a period novel, you think, but do not say."},{"k":"n","t":"He lets a silence sit. Then, eyes on the ceiling again, in a tone too idle to be idle:"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Tell me something. If you were me—if you came face to face with the person who’d once hurt you, badly—what would you do.”"},{"k":"n","t":"**Choice**"},{"k":"n","t":"A. Say you’d forgive them—maybe they had their reasons."},{"k":"n","t":"B. Say you’d make them pay it back, with interest."},{"k":"n","t":"A"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Maybe they had their reasons. I think I’d forgive them.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Forgive.” He turns the word over like he’s found it on the bottom of his shoe. “How saintly of you.” His hand closes on the problem set, and he makes as if to tear it clean in half. “You can’t do this paper anyway. I’ll spare us both—find your own questions.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You lunge for it. He whips it up out of reach, eyebrow raised, waiting."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Fine—they’d had their reasons, but they still hurt me. So I’d make them pay it back. Properly.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"The paper comes down. “…Better.” Something in him is satisfied in a way that has nothing to do with the answer being correct."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"B"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’d make them pay it back. With interest. Reasons or no reasons—they still did it.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"His eyebrow lifts. “Pay it back.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Pay it back.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He looks at you a moment longer than the answer warranted, and the thing in his face is not disapproval. It’s something closer to recognition, and he files it, and does not explain it."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"What follows is forty minutes of you rubbing the cramp out of his shoulder and then his calf at his lordship’s languid instruction—there, no, lower, you call that pressure—on the grounds that you “owe him,” a debt whose terms expand every time you think you’ve paid it. By the time he finally deigns to look at your actual problem, your arm aches and your patience is a thread."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Take the paper home. Finish it. Bring it back tomorrow for marking.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“That’s it? That’s the tutoring? No notes, no worked examples, no—anything?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"A cold sniff. “I have notes.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Then where—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Threw them out.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Why?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"He turns his face to the window, supremely unbothered. “Got stood up on a Friday. Wasn’t in the mood.”"},{"k":"n","t":"And there is, you find, absolutely nothing to say to that. You stuff the paper into your bag and leave, jaw tight, and you are at the door when his voice catches you one more time—"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“Tomorrow. Four o’clock.” A pause. “Not twenty-three minutes past.”"},{"k":"n","t":"—and you take the bus back to the dorm with a heart condition’s worth of new information, a cramped arm, and a suspicion you can’t put down, turning the silver ring and the capsized boat and the torn song over and over the whole way home."}]},{"n":24,"title":"The Introduction","lead":"jael","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"### The café below the hospital"},{"k":"n","t":"Jael picks the place, and the place tells you something: not a hotel bar, not somewhere with a wine list you couldn’t pronounce, but a small steamed-bun café across the road from St. Remedy, the kind with laminated menus and a television no one watches, the kind of place a girl who counts bus fare can sit in without performing. It is, you understand, a courtesy. It is also a strategy. A man who wanted to overwhelm you would have chosen marble. A man who wanted you to lower your guard chooses dumplings under fluorescent light, and waits to see if you notice the difference."},{"k":"n","t":"You notice. You sit down anyway."},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"“Your brother’s scans came back from the second imaging cycle.” He slides a folder across the formica, unhurried, and lets you open it yourself rather than narrating it at you. “The team is cautiously encouraged. I won’t use a stronger word than the data earns, and the data doesn’t yet earn a stronger one. But the pathways they were worried had closed for good are showing the faintest response to stimulation. It is not waking. It is the possibility of the possibility of waking. I thought you’d rather hear it from a person than a portal.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You read the folder twice. The medical language you’ve taught yourself over fourteen months resolves into the one sentence under all of it: not nothing. Not yet, but not nothing. Your hands are steadier than your chest."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Why are you the one telling me this. The foundation has staff for this.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"“It does.” He stirs a tea he isn’t drinking. “I find I’d rather do some things myself. You’ll decide that means something or it doesn’t—you decide that about everything, I’ve noticed.” A faint smile, warm at the surface and unreadable underneath, the way still water is warm on top and cold all the way down. “You don’t have to trust it. You just have to use it. Your brother doesn’t care about my motives any more than he cares about mine.”"},{"k":"n","t":"It is, you’ll admit, well done. He has handed you hope for Kye and asked for nothing visible in return, and the not-asking sits in your stomach like a held breath, because in your experience the people who ask for nothing visible are the ones whose bill arrives latest and largest."},{"k":"n","t":"### The room with the people worth knowing"},{"k":"n","t":"He mentions it as you’re leaving, light, an afterthought that is not an afterthought."},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"“There’s a dinner on Thursday. Private room, a few people who fund the kind of research your brother’s trial lives on—the sort of room where the next imaging cycle gets approved or doesn’t, depending on whether the right person remembers it fondly.” He holds the café door for you. “I could bring a guest. You’d learn more about how Kye’s world actually works in two hours in that room than in a year of reading portals. Or you could not come, and let strangers decide his funding without ever having seen your face. Your call. I only open the door.”"},{"k":"n","t":"A room with the people who decide whether your brother’s treatment continues. You do the arithmetic in the time it takes him to hold a door, and there is only one answer, and he knew that when he asked."},{"k":"n","t":"You go."},{"k":"n","t":"⸻"},{"k":"n","t":"The room is everything the café was not—a private dining room above the river, the kind of money that doesn’t announce itself because it has never once doubted it would be obeyed. Jael moves you through it with a hand never quite touching your back, naming people you file instantly: the foundation chair, a hospital board member, a woman whose family endows the neuroscience wing. You are out of your depth and you do not let it show, and Jael, beside you, makes the not-showing easier, supplying a name a half-second before you’d need it, smoothing your unfamiliarity into something that reads as composure."},{"k":"n","t":"And then one of them decides to find out what you are."},{"k":"n","t":"Board member"},{"k":"n","t":"An older man, glass in hand, who has clocked the cut of your borrowed dress and the newness of you. “And who’s this, Jael? She doesn’t look like foundation. Let me guess—” a smile with a blade folded into it, pitched for the room “—a scholarship case? One of the little hardship stories we fund so we can feel warm at galas? Tell me, dear, do you even understand what’s discussed in a room like this, or are you just here to be grateful?”"},{"k":"n","t":"The room’s attention swings to you, amused, waiting. This is the oldest game the rich play, and you know it cold from a café two terms ago—make the one without money do a small humiliating thing, and watch whether she has the standing to refuse. But this room is not a café. This man can, with one word in the right ear, ensure Kye’s next imaging cycle is quietly deprioritised into oblivion. There is no flat white to pour over him here. The wrong move doesn’t just cost you face. It costs your brother."},{"k":"n","t":"**Choice**"},{"k":"n","t":"A. Cut him down—show the room you understand the research better than he does, and refuse to be small."},{"k":"n","t":"B. Hold your ground without provoking him, and leave Jael the opening to step in on your behalf."},{"k":"n","t":"C. Lower your head, agree you’re just grateful to be here, and give him the small soft thing he wants."},{"k":"n","t":"A"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I understand it well enough to know the second imaging cycle uses diffusion-weighted sequencing, which your foundation underfunded last quarter—so perhaps the question isn’t whether I belong in the conversation.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You are correct, and being correct in this room is the single most expensive thing you have ever done. The man’s smile freezes, then sets. He does not argue. He simply decides, with the serene cruelty of a person who has never been told no, that a hardship case who embarrassed him in front of the board will not be embarrassing anyone for much longer—and somewhere in the following weeks, quietly, untraceably, Kye’s trial slips a tier in priority."},{"k":"n","t":"END."},{"k":"n","t":"B"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I understand enough to know what’s at stake in it, which is more than most people who’ve never needed it can say.” You say it level, unbothered, neither swallowing it nor sharpening it into a weapon he can punish—and you leave a half-second of silence, deliberate, open."},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"He fills it, smooth as oil over water, a hand coming to rest at the small of your back at last. “She reads the trial data herself, Gerald—which is more than I can say for half this board. I brought her because she’s the rarest thing in this room: someone who actually understands what the money does.” The blade in it is wrapped in such warmth that Gerald can’t take offence without looking like the small one. The room laughs, the moment turns, and you have been lifted clear of the water by a hand you did not have to ask for."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"C"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“I’m just grateful to be here, honestly—I won’t pretend to follow all of it.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The man’s smile widens, satisfied, and he turns away having gotten exactly the small soft thing he came for, and the room files you as what he named you: a hardship case who knows her place. Jael’s expression doesn’t change, but something in it dims—he brought a person to be impressive, and watched her choose to be furniture, and a man who collects assets has just watched one depreciate."},{"k":"n","t":"END."},{"k":"n","t":"The rest of the evening, you stay close to him, because the room has taught you in one stroke what it is: a place where you cannot survive on your own, where your competence is a liability without a powerful hand to frame it as a virtue, and where that hand belongs, tonight and apparently always, to Jael Wen. You hate the lesson. You learn it anyway. By the end of the night the board member who tried to cut you is asking your opinion on the imaging protocol, because Jael made it safe for him to, and Kye’s name has been spoken warmly in a room that decides such things—because Jael spoke it."},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"He walks you out to a car you didn’t order. “You held your nerve in there. Most people I bring either fold or overreach. You did neither—you waited for the door and walked through it.” He opens the car door himself. “I can keep opening them. For you, for your brother. That’s all this is. A series of doors.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“Nothing’s ever just a series of doors.” You meet his eyes. “What’s the toll?”"},{"k":"s","who":"Jael","t":"The still-water smile. “When the toll comes due, you’ll be glad you took the doors first. That’s the only honest thing I can tell you.” He stands aside to let you into the car. “Go home. Your brother’s getting better. Let that be enough for one night.”"},{"k":"n","t":"You take the car. You let it be enough for one night, because Kye’s pathways are responding and a roomful of strangers said his name kindly and you are too tired to price the doors right now."},{"k":"n","t":"⸻"},{"k":"n","t":"You do not see her. You have no reason to—you don’t know she exists."},{"k":"n","t":"But across the street from the restaurant, half in the shadow of an awning, a girl who has been following you since the autumn intensive watches Jael Wen hand you into a private car outside one of the most exclusive rooms in the city, his hand at your back, his head bent to yours. She doesn’t know who he is. She doesn’t need to. She knows what it looks like, and she files the image the way you file everything—carefully, against a day she might need it."},{"k":"n","t":"You ride home thinking about Kye. She walks home thinking about you."}]},{"n":25,"title":"The Exam","lead":"shiro","blocks":[{"k":"n","t":"Finals fall the week before the long break, and you walk in carrying two days of Shiro’s brutal, brilliant tutoring and the unfamiliar sensation of being prepared. His paper—the one he made you do and bring back for marking, the one with no notes because he’d thrown them out in a fit of being stood up—turns out to have predicted the exam almost line for line. You sit down already half-certain you’ve passed."},{"k":"n","t":"But the morning has a question in it that has nothing to do with the paper, and you catch yourself, between revising, hoping for one of two things."},{"k":"n","t":"**Choice**"},{"k":"n","t":"Whose presence are you quietly hoping for on exam day?"},{"k":"n","t":"A. Hosea—sharing the same hall, a glance across the rows of desks."},{"k":"n","t":"B. Shiro—turning up afterward, cold-faced, “just to check your answers.”"},{"k":"n","t":"A"},{"k":"n","t":"[Hosea +5]"},{"k":"n","t":"He’s in your hall. You spot him three rows up and over, and once—only once, between sections—he turns his head and finds you across forty bent necks, and holds it for exactly as long as the autumn forum, and you feel it in your spine like a hand laid flat there. Neither of you smiles. Neither of you needs to. The invigilator calls time and the look breaks, but it carries you through the last section steadier than the tutoring did."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"B"},{"k":"n","t":"[Shiro +5]"},{"k":"n","t":"You hand in your booklet and you’re barely through the door when a desk two down from you gets rapped twice, and you turn to find Shiro Si standing there as though he materialised out of the cold—which, given that he is a director and not a candidate and has no earthly business in an exam hall, he essentially did."},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"“The last question. What did you get.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Every head in the corridor turns. You are, the two of you, already a rumour with legs, and he has just walked into a public hall to ask you about a maths problem in front of everyone."},{"k":"s","who":"Sealyra","t":"“…Twenty-four.”"},{"k":"s","who":"Shiro","t":"A pause. The corner of his mouth tips, almost nothing. “Mm. Twenty-four.” A beat. “Adequately intelligent, then. For once.”"},{"k":"n","t":"He turns and goes before you can answer, leaving the corridor buzzing and you standing there with a warmth you decline to examine."},{"k":"n","t":"Continue."},{"k":"n","t":"You walk out into the bright cold with a finished exam and the quiet certainty that, for the first time since you arrived at Concord, you were not behind. You think about Kye’s scans, the faint response in the closed pathways. You think about a borrowed dress and a private car and a series of doors with an unnamed toll. You think about a silver ring and a capsized boat. You have a great many open threads and, for one walking-home moment, you let yourself not pull on any of them."},{"k":"n","t":"You do not know that one of them is, at this exact moment, being pulled by someone else."},{"k":"n","t":"⸻"},{"k":"n","t":"Across campus, in an empty classroom, Hosea Su has asked Faye to stay behind."},{"k":"n","t":"He does not raise his voice. He sets his phone on the desk between them and plays, without comment, a stretch of corridor footage: Faye leaving a washroom, falling into step a careful distance behind a girl in a yellow coat, following her through three turns of campus and out toward the marina shuttle. He lets it run to the end. He plays a second clip: Faye, cap pulled low, slipping into an empty classroom, tearing a stack of notes off a desk, kicking the chair on her way out."},{"k":"n","t":"He says nothing. He simply watches her watch herself."},{"k":"s","who":"Faye","t":"“I—I just admire her. I wanted to be her friend, that’s all, I followed her because—” The story comes apart in her mouth even as she builds it. “The notes weren’t—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He turns the phone face-down. When he finally speaks, it is quiet, and worse for being quiet. “St. Mary’s holds itself to a standard of conduct between students. Stalking. Vandalism. And what nearly happened on that boat, which I have not shown you but which I have. I’ve already filed it with the dean’s office. The decision comes next week.” He gathers his phone. “You should prepare your parents. They’ll want some warning before the letter.”"},{"k":"n","t":"The word parents hits her like a dropped floor. She is across the room and gripping his sleeve before she’s decided to move, nails biting his skin."},{"k":"s","who":"Faye","t":"“No—please—there has to be something—”"},{"k":"s","who":"Hosea","t":"He frowns, moves to pull free of her—"},{"k":"s","who":"Faye","t":"“—I know something about her.” The words tumble out, desperate, fast. “About your precious Sealyra. I followed her, I told you—I followed her to a restaurant. A private one. The kind people like us don’t get into without a name.” Her eyes are wet and bright and frantic. “She wasn’t with you. She was with a man. He had his hand on her back. He put her into his car.”"},{"k":"n","t":"Hosea goes very still."}]}]}};
